window.MH_TR["ch00"]={"t":"The Drawing as a Contract","l":[[1.0,"On the table lies a drawing of a single part: several views,"],[6.0,"dozens of dimensions, symbols, notes and a title block."],[11.0,"Most people see it as a picture of the part."],[14.0,"Someone who reads drawings at a high level sees something completely different: a contract."],[20.0,"In this course we will learn to read that contract all the way through:"],[25.0,"what it requires, what it allows, and what it does not say at all."],[30.0,"Every drawing has at least three parties who read it."],[34.0,"The designer writes in it what the part must do."],[37.0,"The manufacturer reads in it what he is allowed to make."],[41.0,"And the inspector reads in it the basis for accepting or rejecting the part."],[46.0,"If all three read the same drawing and understand the same thing, the drawing has done its job."],[52.0,"If each one understands something different, the argument will"],[56.0,"come in the end, usually when the parts are already finished."],[62.0,"A part that meets every requirement on the drawing is a conforming part,"],[66.0,"even if it does not work in the assembly."],[69.0,"And a part that exceeds a single requirement, even by a hundredth"],[74.0,"of a millimeter, is a rejected part, even if it works perfectly."],[79.0,"That sounds harsh, but it is the principle that lets a manufacturer on"],[82.0,"one side of the world make a part that will be assembled on the other."],[87.0,"So when a conforming part does not work, the problem is"],[90.0,"in the drawing, and the drawing is what gets corrected."],[94.0,"And when a rejected part still has to be used, that is a formal decision by"],[99.0,"whoever is responsible for the design, not a decision of the shop floor."],[106.0,"One of the basic rules in ASME Y14.5 is that the drawing"],[110.0,"defines the part, without dictating how to make it."],[114.0,"The drawing says that a hole must be ten millimeters in diameter,"],[118.0,"with a certain tolerance and at a certain location."],[121.0,"It does not say whether to drill it, mill it or ream it."],[125.0,"The decision of how to make it belongs to the manufacturer. That is what lets"],[130.0,"him choose the cheapest and fastest process that still meets the requirements."],[136.0,"There are exceptions: when the process itself determines the properties of the part,"],[141.0,"such as heat treatment, coating or welding, the drawing refers to a specification that defines it."],[148.0,"A good drawing reader asks about every requirement:"],[151.0,"does it define the part, or does it dictate a method to me?"],[156.0,"And a method is binding only when it is written on the drawing explicitly."],[161.0,"One of the things that sets an advanced reader apart is that he also reads what is not written."],[168.0,"A dimension with no tolerance next to it is not a dimension without a tolerance."],[173.0,"It takes the general tolerance from the title block or from a general note."],[178.0,"All dimensions on the drawing apply at a temperature of"],[181.0,"twenty degrees Celsius, even if that is not written anywhere."],[185.0,"They apply to the part in its free state, without clamping, unless stated otherwise."],[192.0,"And the standard named in the title block defines dozens of rules"],[195.0,"that are not drawn, and those rules differ from standard to standard."],[200.0,"That is why exactly the same drawing can be interpreted differently under"],[205.0,"ASME and under ISO, and we will see this in detail later in the course."],[211.0,"Another basic rule: you do not measure dimensions from the picture itself."],[216.0,"Even when the drawing looks accurately to scale, a dimension that is not"],[221.0,"written on it is not defined, and you must not fill it in with a ruler."],[226.0,"And if a dimension needed for manufacturing is missing,"],[230.0,"that is not an invitation to guess. It is a question for the designer."],[236.0,"A good drawing defines every characteristic of the part once, in one way, and without contradictions."],[243.0,"A dangerous drawing is not necessarily a drawing with an obvious mistake."],[248.0,"Often it is a drawing that can be read in two ways."],[251.0,"A duplicated dimension, an undefined surface, a note that contradicts a dimension,"],[257.0,"or a tolerance that cannot be measured: each one is an opening for an argument."],[263.0,"A large part of this course is aimed at exactly that: spotting these openings before they cost money."],[270.0,"The course is built in six parts."],[273.0,"In the first part we revisit representation itself at an advanced level:"],[278.0,"projection systems, sections, conventions and spatial thinking."],[283.0,"In the second part we go deep into dimensions and tolerances:"],[287.0,"dimensioning philosophy, general tolerances,"],[291.0,"the envelope principle versus the independency principle, fits and tolerance stack-ups."],[298.0,"In the third part we read geometric tolerances within the complete drawing,"],[302.0,"through the eyes of the datum reference frame."],[305.0,"In the fourth part we cover everything beyond geometry: surface texture,"],[311.0,"welding, material, heat treatment, coating, threads and special features."],[317.0,"In the fifth part we read the drawing as a document: the title block and notes,"],[323.0,"assembly drawings, critical characteristics, model-based definition and change management."],[331.0,"And in the sixth part we bring it all together: we read drawings through the manufacturer's eyes,"],[337.0,"find errors, and build a fixed reading method for any drawing."],[343.0,"The basis of the course is ASME Y14.5 from 2018, the standard most common in industry."],[350.0,"Wherever ISO differs from it in a significant way, we will stop and look at"],[355.0,"the difference, because in real work drawings arrive under both standards."],[360.0,"All the drawings in the course were built specifically for it."],[364.0,"They are engineering-correct parts, but they do not belong to any product or company."],[371.0,"A drawing is a contract between the designer, the manufacturer and the inspector."],[377.0,"It defines the part, not the process; it is binding even in what"],[381.0,"it does not say; and you do not measure from it with a ruler."],[386.0,"In the next chapter we start from the basis of every drawing:"],[390.0,"how a three-dimensional body is transferred"],[392.0,"onto a flat sheet, and why confusing the projection angle can turn an entire part into its mirror image."],[399.0,"See you in chapter one."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch01"]={"t":"Projection Systems","l":[[1.0,"A mechanical part is three-dimensional, and the sheet is flat."],[6.0,"Every drawing starts with one question: how do you transfer"],[9.0,"the part onto the sheet without losing any information?"],[13.0,"The answer is orthographic projection, and two different systems for arranging the views."],[20.0,"In this chapter we will understand both of them in depth,"],[23.0,"and see how confusing them produces a part that is the mirror image of what was designed."],[30.0,"In orthographic projection, the lines of sight are parallel"],[34.0,"to each other and perpendicular to the projection plane."],[37.0,"So every view shows only two dimensions: width and height, or width and depth, and never all three."],[44.0,"A surface parallel to the projection plane appears in its true size and shape."],[50.0,"A surface perpendicular to the plane appears only as a line."],[55.0,"And an inclined surface appears foreshortened: it looks smaller than it really is."],[61.0,"That is another reason not to measure from the picture:"],[64.0,"a length that appears on the sheet is not necessarily the true length."],[70.0,"Imagine the part inside a glass box, with one view projected onto each of its six sides."],[76.0,"When the box is unfolded onto the sheet, there are two ways to arrange the views,"],[81.0,"and that is the whole difference between the two systems."],[85.0,"In third-angle projection, common in the United States and in ASME standards,"],[91.0,"the projection plane is between the eye and the part."],[96.0,"So each view is drawn on the side it is viewed from:"],[100.0,"the top view above the front view, and the right- side view to its right."],[106.0,"In first-angle projection, common in Europe and in ISO standards,"],[110.0,"the part is between the eye and the projection plane."],[115.0,"Then each view is drawn on the opposite side:"],[118.0,"the top view below the front view, and the right- side view to its left."],[124.0,"systems. Only their position on the sheet is different."],[131.0,"And someone who knows only one system will read the other one backwards, without noticing."],[137.0,"To remove any doubt, the title block includes a symbol that defines the system."],[143.0,"The symbol is a truncated cone in two views: a side view,"],[147.0,"which looks like a trapezoid, and an end view, which looks like two circles."],[153.0,"The two circles are the view from the narrow end of the cone."],[157.0,"In third angle, the circles are drawn next to the narrow end, and the trapezoid points toward them."],[163.0,"the wide end, and the trapezoid points away from them."],[170.0,"That is how you identify the system correctly,"],[173.0,"even if the symbol was drawn mirrored or in an unexpected place."],[178.0,"Sometimes, instead of a symbol, the system is written out in words."],[184.0,"Suppose a first-angle drawing arrives, and it is read by someone used to third angle."],[190.0,"The part is a plate with a tall wall along the back edge, and a hole near the front right corner."],[196.0,"In first angle, the top view is drawn below the front view,"],[200.0,"and the side closest to the front view is the back of the part."],[204.0,"bottom view, and understands the near side as the front."],[211.0,"The result: the wall moves to the front, and the hole moves to the back."],[218.0,"The part that is made is the mirror image of what was designed."],[224.0,"And the dangerous part: the views stay consistent with each other,"],[229.0,"so nothing on the drawing looks wrong."],[232.0,"nothing. On a non-symmetrical part it scraps the entire batch."],[240.0,"What do you do when a drawing arrives with no symbol and no note?"],[244.0,"First check the title block and the general notes."],[248.0,"Sometimes the system is defined there, or in the standard the drawing refers to."],[254.0,"Then check whether a 3D model is attached to the drawing, and compare against it."],[260.0,"But do not rely on the views looking illogical. As we saw,"],[264.0,"the reversed interpretation is completely consistent."],[268.0,"And if there is still doubt, ask the designer."],[272.0,"On a non-symmetrical part, a guess is a bet on the entire batch."],[278.0,"The front view does not have to be the front of the part. It is the view that describes the shape best."],[285.0,"It is chosen so that it has the fewest hidden lines,"],[288.0,"usually in the orientation in which the part is assembled or machined."],[293.0,"The number of views is the minimum that defines the part completely."],[297.0,"A turned part, such as a shaft, is often defined in a single view,"],[302.0,"and the diameter symbol replaces the end view."],[305.0,"An unnecessary view is not just wasted space."],[308.0,"It is one more place where dimensions can contradict each other."],[314.0,"What happens when a part has an inclined surface that"],[317.0,"is not parallel to any of the three principal planes?"],[321.0,"In all the regular views it appears foreshortened, and a hole on it looks like an ellipse."],[327.0,"Here we use an auxiliary view: a view projected onto a plane parallel to the inclined surface."],[334.0,"In the auxiliary view the surface appears in its true size and shape, and the hole looks round."],[341.0,"The auxiliary view is drawn in line with the direction of sight onto the surface,"],[346.0,"from the view in which the surface appears as a line."],[350.0,"a partial auxiliary view, so as not to clutter the drawing."],[359.0,"When a full view is not needed, a partial view is drawn, cut off with a break line."],[365.0,"A local view shows only one feature, such as a keyway or a slot, without drawing the whole part."],[372.0,"A detail view marks a small area with a circle and a letter,"],[377.0,"and draws it separately at a larger scale, for example two to one."],[383.0,"And the dimensions in the detail view are still the true dimensions, not the enlarged ones."],[388.0,"A view moved from its natural position must be identified: an arrow with a letter"],[394.0,"that shows the viewing direction, and the same letter next to the view itself."],[399.0,"A view that was moved without being identified is an error"],[403.0,"in the drawing, because it breaks the rules of projection."],[408.0,"Orthographic projection shows two dimensions in each view,"],[411.0,"and inclined surfaces appear foreshortened."],[415.0,"In third angle the view is drawn on the side it is viewed from. In first angle, on the opposite side."],[422.0,"Before reading a single view, check the projection symbol, and if there is no symbol, ask."],[428.0,"In the next chapter we will cut the part: advanced sections,"],[432.0,"and what each type of section reveals and hides."],[436.0,"See you in chapter two."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch02"]={"t":"Advanced Sections","l":[[1.0,"A regular view shows the part from the outside. Everything inside appears as hidden lines."],[8.0,"In a complex part, the hidden lines pile up on top of each other, and they cannot be read."],[14.0,"The section view solves this: the part is cut by an imaginary plane,"],[19.0,"the near portion is removed, and we look inside."],[23.0,"In this chapter we will get to know the types of sections,"],[26.0,"their conventions, and what each section reveals and what it hides."],[32.0,"Every section starts with the cutting-plane line, drawn in the adjacent view."],[37.0,"It shows where the cutting plane passes, and the arrows at its ends show the viewing direction."],[43.0,"The letters at the ends identify the section, for example A,"],[48.0,"and the section itself is called section A-A."],[52.0,"line. Without it, you do not know what you are looking at."],[59.0,"The direction of the arrows determines what remains in the view:"],[64.0,"the portion the eye sees, not the portion that was removed."],[70.0,"A cut surface is marked with hatching: thin parallel lines, usually at 45 degrees."],[76.0,"What lies behind the cutting plane is drawn with regular lines,"],[80.0,"and what is hidden is usually not drawn at all."],[84.0,"A single part has the same hatching in every section on the drawing."],[89.0,"In an assembly, adjacent parts get hatching at a different angle or a different spacing,"],[94.0,"so it is clear where one part ends and the next begins."],[99.0,"The hatch pattern does not define the material."],[102.0,"The material is defined in the title block or in the parts list."],[107.0,"And a very thin part, such as a gasket or sheet metal,"],[111.0,"is sometimes shown solid filled instead of hatched."],[116.0,"In a full section, the plane passes through the entire part, usually along the axis of symmetry."],[123.0,"On a symmetrical part you can save effort: a half section removes only a"],[127.0,"quarter of the part, and the view shows half cut and half from the outside."],[132.0,"That way one picture shows both the external shape and the internal one."],[137.0,"The boundary between the two halves is a center line,"],[141.0,"not a solid line, because there is no real edge there."],[145.0,"And on the uncut half, hidden lines are not drawn, because the cut half already shows them."],[153.0,"Sometimes the important features do not lie in the same plane."],[157.0,"In an offset section, the cutting-plane line changes direction"],[161.0,"at right angles and passes through all the features."],[165.0,"In the section itself everything is drawn as if it were cut in a single plane,"],[169.0,"and the bends in the cutting-plane line are not shown."],[173.0,"So someone who reads the section without checking the"],[176.0,"cutting-plane line may think all the features lie in one row."],[181.0,"On a round part, such as a flange, the holes usually sit on a bolt circle, not opposite each other."],[188.0,"In an aligned section, the cutting-plane line passes through the holes at an angle,"],[193.0,"and the inclined portion is then rotated into the plane."],[197.0,"That way the holes appear in the section at their true distance from the center."],[202.0,"And this is an important point: such a section is not a true projection."],[207.0,"It deliberately departs from the rules of projection to show what matters."],[213.0,"A flange with three holes, 120 degrees apart,"],[216.0,"will look in the section as if it had holes on both sides of the center."],[221.0,"The number of holes and their angular position are read in the front view, not in the section."],[228.0,"Some things are not hatched even when the plane passes through them."],[232.0,"A rib or a thin web cut lengthwise is not hatched,"],[236.0,"because hatching would give the impression of a solid, heavy part."],[241.0,"nuts, pins, keys and balls are not sectioned lengthwise."],[248.0,"They are drawn whole, even when the plane passes exactly through their axis."],[254.0,"Someone who does not know this convention may think the"],[257.0,"bolt was not cut at all, or that the rib does not exist."],[261.0,"And on a part with ribs or spokes that are not on the cutting-plane line,"],[265.0,"they too are rotated into the plane, as in an aligned section."],[271.0,"When only a small internal area needs to be shown, a broken-out section is used."],[277.0,"A small portion of the view is removed and bounded by a freehand break line."],[283.0,"A revolved section shows the cross-sectional shape of a long feature,"],[287.0,"such as a spoke or an arm. It is rotated ninety degrees and drawn on the view itself."],[294.0,"A removed section is the same idea, but it is drawn outside the view,"],[299.0,"identified with letters, and sometimes at a larger scale."],[305.0,"A section reveals the inside, but only where the plane passed."],[309.0,"A hole outside the plane will not appear in the section."],[313.0,"And since hidden lines are not drawn, it will not appear as a hidden line either."],[319.0,"So a section is never a complete picture. It is read together with the other views."],[326.0,"And when a feature seems to be missing, first check whether it simply"],[330.0,"does not lie in the cutting plane, before assuming it does not exist."],[336.0,"Before every section: find the cutting-plane line and the direction of the arrows."],[342.0,"they were in the same plane, even when they are not."],[349.0,"Ribs, shafts and bolts cut lengthwise are not hatched, and that is a convention, not a mistake."],[356.0,"And whatever is not in the plane does not appear in the section."],[360.0,"The section complements the views; it does not replace them."],[364.0,"In the next chapter we will get to know the conventions and simplifications of the drawing:"],[371.0,"threads, gears, springs, repeated features, and where the drawing simplifies on purpose."],[378.0,"See you in chapter three."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch03"]={"t":"Conventions and Simplifications","l":[[1.0,"A drawing does not try to be an accurate picture of the part."],[5.0,"Accurately drawing every thread form, every gear tooth or every"],[9.0,"coil of a spring would take hours, and would add no information."],[13.0,"So the drawing uses conventions: a shortened,"],[16.0,"agreed representation that describes the feature without drawing it."],[21.0,"In this chapter we will get to know the important conventions, and the principle behind all of them:"],[27.0,"when the picture is simplified, the information is in the callout."],[33.0,"On an external thread, according to ISO, the major diameter is"],[38.0,"drawn with a thick line and the root diameter with a thin line."],[43.0,"In the end view, the root diameter is drawn as three quarters of a thin circle,"],[49.0,"so it will not be confused with a hole or a chamfer."],[53.0,"A thick line marks the end of the full thread."],[57.0,"In ASME standards, in the simplified representation, the root diameter is sometimes"],[62.0,"shown as a hidden line, so you need to know which standard the drawing follows."],[68.0,"In all cases, the picture does not define the thread."],[73.0,"The callout defines it: type, diameter, pitch and tolerance class."],[80.0,"On an internal thread the picture is reversed: the tap drill diameter,"],[85.0,"which is the minor diameter, is drawn with a thick line, and the major diameter with a thin line."],[93.0,"In a section, the hatching goes up to the thick line, that is, up to the wall of the hole."],[100.0,"In a blind hole, both the thread depth and the drill"],[103.0,"depth are drawn, and the drill depth is always deeper."],[107.0,"And the drill point is drawn as a cone at 120 degrees, even if the actual drill angle is 118."],[115.0,"The depth of the hole is measured to the end of the full diameter, not to the tip of the cone."],[122.0,"assembly is drawn in section, the two threads meet."],[129.0,"the bolt is drawn in full, and it hides the internal thread."],[136.0,"The internal thread appears only in the part of the hole where there is no bolt,"],[141.0,"and there the hatching again goes up to the thick line."],[144.0,"Someone who does not know this may think the hole is not threaded to its full depth."],[151.0,"In the conventional representation, a gear is drawn without teeth:"],[155.0,"the addendum circle with a thick line, and the pitch circle with a thin center line."],[162.0,"Sometimes one or two teeth are drawn, when the position of a tooth"],[166.0,"relative to another feature, such as a keyway or a hole, must be shown."],[172.0,"And in drawings produced from CAD systems, very often all the teeth are drawn."],[178.0,"But even then, the tooth shape in the picture is only a picture."],[182.0,"You do not measure from it, and it does not define the tooth."],[186.0,"What defines the teeth is in a data table on the drawing: module,"],[192.0,"number of teeth, pressure angle, accuracy grade and inspection dimensions."],[200.0,"from the drawing, even if the picture looks complete."],[208.0,"Splines are usually drawn in a simplified representation, like gears, and their data"],[214.0,"appears in a table or in a callout according to the standard, for example ISO 4156."],[222.0,"Rolling bearings on an assembly drawing are sometimes drawn"],[226.0,"as a rectangle with a cross inside, according to ISO 8826."],[232.0,"The bearing itself is defined by its catalog number in the parts list, not by the picture."],[239.0,"A spring is also drawn simplified: a few coils at the ends, and the rest shown with center lines."],[246.0,"The spring is defined in a table: wire diameter, mean diameter,"],[250.0,"number of coils, free length, and sometimes a force at a given length."],[256.0,"On a spring, the force at a given length is often more important than any geometric dimension."],[264.0,"When the same feature repeats several times, it is drawn and defined once."],[269.0,"A callout such as 6X @10 means there are six identical holes."],[275.0,"At the other locations only center lines are drawn, and sometimes not even the holes themselves."],[282.0,"The number of occurrences in the callout is what is binding. If the picture shows"],[287.0,"five centers and the callout says six, there is a contradiction, and you ask."],[294.0,"Sometimes EQS, or EQ SP, is added to the callout, meaning equally spaced."],[300.0,"When this callout appears, an angular dimension for each hole is not needed."],[305.0,"Without it, the angle between the holes must be defined by a dimension."],[311.0,"On a symmetrical part, only half, or even a quarter, can be drawn."],[316.0,"According to ISO, two short parallel lines at the ends of the"],[320.0,"center line indicate that the part is symmetrical about it."],[324.0,"A long part with a constant cross-section,"],[327.0,"such as a shaft or a profile, is drawn shortened using break lines."],[332.0,"The dimension written on it is the true length, not the drawn length."],[337.0,"from the picture will get a completely wrong number."],[343.0,"A flat surface on a round shaft is marked with two thin"],[346.0,"crossing lines, to distinguish it from a round surface."],[350.0,"Knurling is drawn on only a small portion of the surface,"],[354.0,"and the type and pitch are defined in the callout."],[358.0,"A small intersection between two features, such as a small hole entering a shaft,"],[363.0,"is sometimes drawn with straight lines instead of the exact curve."],[368.0,"sometimes drawn with a thin line, or not drawn at all."],[376.0,"And in ASME standards, hidden lines are drawn only when they are needed."],[381.0,"The absence of a hidden line does not prove there is nothing there."],[387.0,"A line of one long dash and two short dashes, a phantom line, shows an alternate"],[392.0,"position of a moving part, or an adjacent part that does not belong to the drawing."],[398.0,"It provides context only, and it is not part of the geometry the drawing defines."],[406.0,"All of these conventions share one principle."],[409.0,"The picture shows where the feature is and roughly what it looks like."],[413.0,"The callout, the table and the note define it exactly."],[418.0,"So when there is a gap between the picture and the callout,"],[421.0,"the callout usually governs, but such a gap is always a reason to ask."],[426.0,"And someone who does not know a convention will see it as an"],[430.0,"error in the drawing, or worse, interpret it as real geometry."],[436.0,"The drawing simplifies on purpose: threads, gears, springs, repeated features and long parts."],[444.0,"On an external thread the major diameter is thick,"],[447.0,"and on an internal thread the tap drill diameter is thick."],[451.0,"Gears and springs are defined in a table,"],[453.0,"and repeated features are defined by the number in the callout."],[458.0,"And wherever the picture is simplified, the information is in the callout, not in the picture."],[464.0,"In the next chapter we move to the second part of the course: dimensioning philosophy,"],[470.0,"and why a good dimension is first of all an engineering decision."],[475.0,"See you in chapter four."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch04"]={"t":"Dimensioning Philosophy","l":[[1.0,"A dimension on a drawing is not just a number. It is an engineering decision."],[7.0,"distance, from which point, and with which tolerance."],[13.0,"Exactly the same part can receive two different dimensioning schemes,"],[17.0,"and end up as two parts that function differently."],[21.0,"In this chapter we will learn to read the intent behind the dimensions, not just the numbers."],[27.0,"A functional dimension directly defines what matters for the function of the part."],[32.0,"If two holes must match two pins on another part,"],[35.0,"the important distance is the distance between them."],[38.0,"So it is dimensioned directly, from hole to hole, and not each hole separately from the edge."],[44.0,"A dimension chosen for manufacturing convenience can harm the function,"],[49.0,"even when all the numbers are within tolerance."],[52.0,"The standard is aimed at functional dimensioning."],[55.0,"The manufacturing method is chosen by the manufacturer later."],[60.0,"There are two main ways to arrange dimensions: as a chain, or from a single baseline."],[66.0,"In chain dimensioning, each dimension starts where the previous one ended."],[71.0,"With four chained dimensions, each with a tolerance of one tenth,"],[75.0,"the position of the last feature can vary by up to four tenths."],[79.0,"In baseline dimensioning, every feature is dimensioned"],[83.0,"from the same edge, and the tolerance does not accumulate."],[87.0,"But there is a price: the distance between two adjacent features is"],[91.0,"no longer controlled directly, and it can vary by up to two tenths."],[96.0,"There is no single right way. The choice tells the"],[99.0,"reader which distance the designer wanted to keep tight."],[103.0,"On drawings for CNC manufacturing you often see ordinate dimensioning:"],[108.0,"one zero point, and each feature gets one number on each axis."],[113.0,"This is really baseline dimensioning, with the same properties: no accumulation from"],[118.0,"the baseline, but the distance between two features is not controlled directly."],[124.0,"a hole table, with a letter or a number for each hole."],[131.0,"and the tolerances apply to it like any other dimension."],[139.0,"A redundant dimension is one that can be calculated from other dimensions on the drawing."],[144.0,"A closed chain is the classic example: three partial dimensions,"],[149.0,"plus an overall dimension that covers all three."],[153.0,"A part can meet three of them and exceed the fourth,"],[156.0,"and then it is not clear which dimension governs."],[159.0,"So a correct chain leaves one segment undimensioned,"],[162.0,"or marks one of the dimensions as a reference dimension."],[166.0,"And the segment left without a dimension is usually the least"],[170.0,"important one. That too is a statement by the designer."],[174.0,"A reference dimension appears in parentheses, and sometimes with the note REF."],[179.0,"It is for information only: it has no tolerance, and it is not inspected for acceptance of the part."],[186.0,"It is there to save the reader a calculation, for example an overall length."],[191.0,"A common mistake is to inspect a reference dimension and reject a part"],[195.0,"because of it. The part is judged by the dimensions that define it."],[200.0,"A basic dimension is enclosed in a frame."],[204.0,"It is a theoretically exact dimension: it has no tolerance of its own,"],[208.0,"and the general tolerance does not apply to it."],[212.0,"The tolerance of the feature comes from its geometric"],[215.0,"feature control frame, for example a position tolerance."],[219.0,"Someone who applies the general tolerance from the title block to a basic"],[224.0,"dimension is inspecting the part against a requirement that does not exist."],[229.0,"And a basic dimension with no geometric tolerance referring to it is an"],[233.0,"error on the drawing, because then the feature has no tolerance at all."],[238.0,"Instead of a dimension and a tolerance, both limits can be written directly,"],[243.0,"for example twenty and twenty point zero five."],[246.0,"This is convenient for manufacturing and inspection, because no calculation is needed."],[252.0,"And some dimensions have only one limit, marked MIN or MAX,"],[256.0,"for example a maximum corner radius or a minimum thread depth."],[261.0,"determined by the function or by other dimensions."],[269.0,"ASME has a symbol called the dimension origin symbol:"],[272.0,"a small circle at the end of the dimension line, instead of an arrowhead."],[278.0,"It says which feature measurement starts from,"],[281.0,"and that changes the result when the surfaces are not perfectly parallel."],[285.0,"Measuring from the wrong side can accept a part that"],[288.0,"should have been rejected, or reject a good part."],[292.0,"When a dimension is changed on the drawing without updating the picture,"],[296.0,"it is marked with a line under the number."],[299.0,"The line says: the picture here does not match the number, and the number governs."],[305.0,"And that is another reminder of the rule from chapter zero: do not measure from the picture."],[311.0,"The dimensioning scheme is where the designer tells you what matters to him."],[315.0,"A direct dimension between two features says: this distance is critical."],[321.0,"Dimensions from a single baseline say: this edge is the reference point of the part."],[327.0,"And a dimension with a particularly tight tolerance says:"],[331.0,"here the function is sensitive, and so is the cost."],[335.0,"Someone who reads the drawing this way can plan manufacturing"],[339.0,"and inspection that protect what really matters."],[343.0,"Let's take an example: a plate with a central hole for a shaft, and two holes for bolts."],[349.0,"The designer dimensioned the bolt holes from the central hole,"],[353.0,"because they must match a flange that sits on the shaft."],[357.0,"The manufacturer would prefer dimensions from the edge,"],[360.0,"because that is where it is convenient for him to fixture and measure."],[364.0,"If the manufacturer converts the dimensions to the edge on his own, the tolerances accumulate,"],[370.0,"and the distance to the central hole can be exceeded"],[373.0,"even when all of his dimensions are within tolerance."],[376.0,"And if he still wants to work from the edge, he must tighten his"],[380.0,"tolerances so that the designer's original dimensions are maintained."],[384.0,"That is the principle: you may manufacture any way you like,"],[388.0,"but inspection is done to the dimensions on the drawing."],[392.0,"A dimension is an engineering decision, not just a number."],[396.0,"A chain accumulates tolerances, a baseline holds each feature relative to the edge,"],[402.0,"and a direct dimension holds the distance between two features."],[406.0,"A redundant dimension creates a contradiction, a reference dimension is not inspected,"],[412.0,"and a basic dimension gets its tolerance only from the feature control frame."],[418.0,"In the next chapter we will talk about the tolerances you cannot see: general tolerances,"],[424.0,"and what they define when there is no tolerance next to a dimension at all."],[429.0,"See you in chapter five."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch05"]={"t":"Default Tolerances","l":[[1.0,"Most dimensions on a drawing are written without a tolerance next to them."],[6.0,"That does not mean they have no tolerance. It means the tolerance is written somewhere else."],[12.0,"Default tolerances govern most of the part's geometry,"],[15.0,"and most readers never look at them at all."],[18.0,"defined, how to read them, and what they do not cover."],[25.0,"Default tolerances appear in the title block or ina general note,"],[30.0,"usually with the words \"unless otherwise specified\"."],[34.0,"They apply to every dimension that has no tolerance of its own."],[38.0,"A dimension with its own tolerance, a reference dimension"],[42.0,"and a basic dimension do not receive the general tolerance."],[47.0,"And if the drawing has no general tolerance at all, dimensions without"],[51.0,"a tolerance are undefined, and that is a question for the designer."],[56.0,"On drawings to ISO, the general tolerance is usually defined by reference to ISO 2768."],[64.0,"Part one of the standard sets tolerances for linear and angular dimensions,"],[72.0,"in four classes: fine, medium, coarse and very coarse, marked f, m, c and v."],[80.0,"The tolerance is not fixed. It grows with the dimension."],[85.0,"In the medium class, a dimension of 20 mm gets +0.2, and a dimension of 200 mm gets +0.5."],[98.0,"without checking which range it falls in on the table."],[103.0,"the shorter leg, not according to the size of the angle."],[110.0,"ISO 2768 also sets tolerances for external radii and chamfer heights:"],[117.0,"in the medium class, up to 3 millimeters +0.2, and above 6 millimeters +1 millimeter."],[126.0,"That way even a broken corner, or a small radius drawn without a tolerance, is defined."],[133.0,"Part two of ISO 2768 set general geometric tolerances: straightness,"],[141.0,"flatness, perpendicularity and symmetry, in three classes: H, K and L."],[150.0,"A callout such as ISO 2768-mK means: medium class for dimensions, and class K for geometry."],[160.0,"In 2021 this second part was replaced by a new standard,"],[164.0,"ISO 22081, so on drawings you can meet both callouts."],[169.0,"Someone who receives a drawing with mK needs to know that the perpendicularity and"],[176.0,"flatness of the part are also limited, even though there is no symbol on them."],[183.0,"ISO 22081 works differently from part two of ISO 2768."],[190.0,"Instead of a table by tolerance type, it usually defines a general profile of a surface tolerance,"],[196.0,"relative to a datum reference frame that appears in the title block."],[201.0,"So when you see a reference to ISO 22081, look in the title"],[206.0,"block for both the tolerance value and the datums it refers to."],[212.0,"On drawings to ASME, and especially on inch drawings, there is a different convention."],[219.0,"The title block defines the tolerance by the number of decimal places."],[229.0,"For example, one place: +0.1. Two places: +0.01. And three places: +0.005."],[240.0,"So the same number, written two ways, gets two different tolerances:"],[247.0,"1.5 versus 1.500 are not the same requirement."],[252.0,"These values are not part of the standard itself. Each company sets them in"],[257.0,"its own title block, so always read the title block of the specific drawing."],[263.0,"And someone who adds or removes a trailing zero on a dimension changes its tolerance."],[268.0,"A typical ASME title block also includes a tolerance for angles,"],[274.0,"for example +0.5\u00b0, and sometimes a note about breaking sharp edges."],[280.0,"And there is a modern trap: a drawing produced from a CAD system,"],[284.0,"with three decimal places on every dimension."],[288.0,"If the title block sets the tolerance by the number of decimal places,"],[292.0,"every such dimension suddenly gets the tightest tolerance."],[297.0,"That is usually not the designer's intent,"],[299.0,"but as far as the drawing is concerned it is the requirement, so you ask."],[305.0,"General tolerances also apply to what is not dimensioned at all."],[309.0,"A right angle drawn without a dimension gets the general angular tolerance."],[314.0,"And holes drawn on the same center line, or features that look symmetrical,"],[319.0,"have that relationship only within the general tolerances, not exactly."],[324.0,"In other words: what looks aligned or perpendicular on the drawing"],[328.0,"is aligned and perpendicular only within the general tolerance."],[333.0,"There is a principle in ISO 2768 worth knowing."],[338.0,"According to the standard, a part that exceeds a general tolerance"],[342.0,"is not automatically rejected if its function is not impaired."],[347.0,"But that is not a decision for whoever is measuring. As we saw in chapter zero,"],[354.0,"the decision on a nonconforming part belongs to whoever is responsible for the design."],[362.0,"ASME Y14.5 has no similar clause. A dimension out of tolerance is out of tolerance."],[370.0,"The general tolerance is chosen according to the normal accuracy of the shop,"],[375.0,"so that most dimensions will be achieved without special effort."],[379.0,"A general tolerance that is too tight makes the whole part more expensive,"],[384.0,"because every dimension becomes a requirement."],[387.0,"And a general tolerance that is too loose hides a risk: an important dimension"],[392.0,"that was left without its own tolerance gets a tolerance that does not suit it."],[398.0,"So when you read a drawing, ask about every important dimension:"],[402.0,"is the general tolerance really enough for it?"],[406.0,"A dimension without a tolerance takes the general tolerance from the title block."],[411.0,"In ISO 2768 the tolerance depends on the size of the dimension,"],[416.0,"and the callout mK also limits the geometry."],[420.0,"On inch drawings, the number of decimal places is part of the requirement."],[425.0,"And what looks perpendicular or aligned on the drawing is limited only by the general tolerance."],[431.0,"In the next chapter we reach one of the deepest differences between ASME and ISO:"],[437.0,"the envelope principle versus the independency principle."],[441.0,"See you in chapter six."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch06"]={"t":"Envelope versus Independency","l":[[1.0,"Suppose a shaft is dimensioned with a diameter of twenty, plus or minus five hundredths."],[8.0,"The shaft is inspected, and every measurement is between twenty minus five"],[13.0,"hundredths and twenty plus five hundredths. But the shaft is slightly bent."],[18.0,"Is it acceptable? The answer depends on the standard in the title block."],[23.0,"Under ASME it may be rejected, and under ISO it may be accepted."],[28.0,"In this chapter we will understand why, and what it changes in inspection and in assembly."],[37.0,"ASME Y14.5 has a rule called Rule #1: the envelope principle."],[44.0,"The rule says that the size limits of a feature govern"],[48.0,"not only its size, but also how much its form may vary."],[52.0,"At maximum material condition, MMC, the feature must have perfect form."],[57.0,"On a shaft, this means the entire shaft must pass through a"],[61.0,"perfect cylinder twenty plus five hundredths in diameter."],[66.0,"The thinner the shaft, the more room it has to bend."],[69.0,"That is allowed, as long as it stays inside the envelope."],[75.0,"So under ASME a feature of size is inspected in two ways."],[80.0,"One check is of the envelope: does the whole part fit into the"],[85.0,"perfect cylinder, or, for a hole, does a perfect pin fit into it."],[91.0,"The second check is of the local size: everywhere along the part,"],[95.0,"the two-point size does not go beyond the other limit."],[99.0,"This is Taylor's long-established principle: a full- form GO gauge, and a two-point NO-GO gauge."],[107.0,"A bent shaft can pass the local measurement at every point,"],[111.0,"and still be rejected in the envelope check."],[115.0,"Take a pin with a diameter of 10 and a tolerance of minus 0.04,"],[126.0,"Under the envelope principle, the largest pin, exactly ten in diameter, must be perfectly straight."],[134.0,"four hundredths, and still stay inside an envelope of ten."],[142.0,"The hole too, at its minimum diameter, must accept a perfect pin ten in diameter."],[148.0,"The result: every conforming pin always fits into every conforming hole."],[154.0,"That is what the envelope principle guarantees."],[158.0,"Under the independency principle, the same pin can measure"],[162.0,"ten at every point and be bent by three hundredths."],[166.0,"Then it occupies a space of 10.03, and may not fit into a hole ten in diameter."],[174.0,"In ISO standards, the default is the opposite."],[178.0,"Under ISO 8015, every requirement on the drawing stands on its own."],[184.0,"A size tolerance controls only the size, not the form."],[188.0,"default between two points, according to ISO 14405."],[196.0,"So our bent shaft meets the size requirement,"],[199.0,"because every two-point measurement is within the limits."],[203.0,"Its bending is limited only by a straightness tolerance,"],[206.0,"if there is one on the drawing, or by the general geometric tolerance."],[212.0,"ISO 14405 does not stop at two points. It defines several"],[217.0,"ways to measure a size, and each one has a modifier."],[222.0,"LP is a two-point size, and it is the default."],[226.0,"GG is a size calculated by the least-squares method,"],[229.0,"the way most coordinate measuring machines calculate."],[233.0,"GN is the smallest cylinder that circumscribes a shaft,"],[237.0,"and GxX is the largest cylinder that fits inside a hole."],[242.0,"The E modifier is in effect a combination:"],[245.0,"GN or GX at the maximum material limit, and LP at the other limit."],[251.0,"And here is an inspection trap: a coordinate measuring machine that calculates a diameter by"],[257.0,"least squares is measuring something other than what the drawing asked for, unless GG is written."],[266.0,"When the function requires an envelope, for example a shaft"],[270.0,"that must fit into a hole, it has to be requested explicitly."],[274.0,"This is done with the letter E in a circle, after the tolerance, for example @20 h7 \u00a9."],[283.0,"Or with a general note, such as ISO 2768-mk-E,"],[287.0,"which applies the envelope to every feature of size on the drawing."],[293.0,"Without the modifier, even a tight ISO 286 fit does not guarantee assembly if the part is bent."],[302.0,"ASME also lets you go in the opposite direction."],[305.0,"which cancels the envelope principle for a specific feature."],[313.0,"On older drawings you will find a note instead, such as PERFECT FORM AT MMC NOT REQUIRED."],[320.0,"And there are cases where the envelope principle does not apply in the first place:"],[327.0,"stock material, such as bars and sheets, and non- rigid parts that are measured in the free state."],[336.0,"It is also important to understand the limit of the envelope principle."],[340.0,"It controls the form of a single feature, but not the relationship between two features."],[347.0,"The perpendicularity of a hole to a surface, or the location of a hole relative to an edge,"],[353.0,"is not controlled by the envelope principle. For that you need geometric tolerances."],[359.0,"Anyone who thinks the size limits ensure that two diameters"],[363.0,"are on the same axis is wrong under both standards."],[368.0,"An ISO drawing read through ASME eyes will lead to rejecting good parts,"],[373.0,"or wasting time on inspections that were not required."],[377.0,"And an ASME drawing read through ISO eyes will lead to"],[381.0,"accepting parts that will not go together in assembly."],[385.0,"So before inspecting a feature of size, find in the title block which standard the drawing follows."],[392.0,"And if the drawing is to ISO and the part has to fit into another part,"],[397.0,"check whether there is an E modifier. If there is not, it is a question for the designer."],[405.0,"In ASME, the default is the envelope principle: at maximum material condition, perfect form."],[412.0,"In ISO standards, the default is the independency principle:"],[416.0,"size is checked between two points, and form is controlled separately."],[422.0,"The E modifier applies an envelope in ISO standards, and the | modifier cancels it in ASME."],[429.0,"And under both standards, the envelope does not control the relationship between features."],[436.0,"In the next chapter we will get to know the ISO 286 system of fits:"],[441.0,"what H7 and g6 mean, and how to read a fit."],[445.0,"See you in chapter seven."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch07"]={"t":"Fits \u00b7 ISO 286","l":[[1.0,"When a shaft goes into a hole, the important question is not only"],[5.0,"what their diameters are, but how they will behave together."],[10.0,"Will the shaft rotate freely, be located precisely, or be pressed in and never move again?"],[17.0,"The ISO 286 system of fits answers this question with two short designations, such as H7 and g6."],[27.0,"In this chapter we will learn to read these designations, calculate the clearance or"],[32.0,"interference from them, and understand what they require of manufacturing and inspection."],[40.0,"Every fit designation is made of a letter and a number."],[43.0,"relative to the nominal size: above it, below it, or around it."],[51.0,"The number is the tolerance grade, IT, and it sets how wide the tolerance zone is."],[58.0,"An upper-case letter denotes a hole, and a lower- case letter denotes a shaft."],[64.0,"So H7 is a hole tolerance, and g6 is a shaft tolerance."],[70.0,"And on an assembly drawing both are written together, with a slash: H7/g6."],[78.0,"Tolerance grades range from IT01, the highest accuracy, up to IT18."],[85.0,"The width of the zone depends on the grade and also on the size:"],[90.0,"the same grade gets a wider zone at a larger diameter."],[94.0,"For diameters between eighteen and thirty millimeters, IT6 is thirteen microns,"],[100.0,"IT7 is twenty-one microns, and IT8 is thirty-three microns."],[105.0,"To get a feel for it: IT6 usually requires grinding or very fine machining,"],[111.0,"IT7 suits reaming or precise milling, and IT8 to IT9 suit normal machining."],[118.0,"Every grade you improve raises the price. That is the economic decision inside the small number."],[126.0,"The letter sets the fundamental deviation: the limit of the tolerance zone closest to the zero line."],[133.0,"For an H hole, the lower limit is exactly the nominal size. The hole can only be larger than it."],[141.0,"nominal size. The shaft can only be smaller than it."],[149.0,"the shaft below the nominal size, and give clearance."],[157.0,"Later letters, such as p or s, raise the shaft above the nominal size, and give interference."],[164.0,"already tells the reader which type of fit is intended."],[173.0,"Most of industry works in the hole-basis system: the hole is always H, and only the shaft changes."],[180.0,"The reason is practical: a precise hole is made with fixed-size tools, such as a reamer,"],[187.0,"and checked with a fixed gauge. It is easier to keep such tools only for H sizes."],[194.0,"The shaft is easier to make and measure at any size, by external machining."],[200.0,"There is also a shaft-basis system, in which the shaft is h."],[205.0,"It is used when one long shaft carries several parts with different fits."],[210.0,"drawing knows he is looking at a hole-basis system."],[218.0,"Take a hole of diameter 25 H7: from exactly 25 up to 25.021."],[226.0,"With a g6 shaft, between 24.980 and 24.993, you get a clearance fit."],[234.0,"The minimum clearance is seven microns, and the maximum is"],[239.0,"forty-one microns. The shaft will always move inside the hole."],[244.0,"With a k6 shaft, between 25.002 and 25.015, you get a transition fit."],[253.0,"In a transition fit, depending on the actual sizes, there can be a clearance"],[258.0,"of up to nineteen microns, or an interference of up to fifteen microns."],[264.0,"Such a fit is chosen for precise location, when the part has to"],[268.0,"be assembled with a light press and taken apart when needed."],[273.0,"With a p6 shaft, between 25.022 and 25.035, you get an interference fit."],[282.0,"The minimum interference is one micron, and the maximum is"],[286.0,"thirty-five microns. The shaft is always larger than the hole."],[291.0,"And the calculation rule is simple: maximum clearance is the largest hole minus the"],[299.0,"smallest shaft, and minimum clearance is the smallest hole minus the largest shaft."],[307.0,"On a part drawing, the designation appears next to the diameter, for example diameter 25 H7."],[315.0,"Many drawings also add the limits themselves in parentheses,"],[318.0,"so nobody has to look them up in a table."],[322.0,"If both the designation and the limits appear,"],[325.0,"and they do not match each other, that is a contradiction, and you ask."],[330.0,"On an assembly drawing the combination appears, H7/g6, to show the intent of the fit."],[339.0,"And on drawings to ASME you sometimes see inch fits of a different kind, such as RC or FN,"],[347.0,"according to ANSI B4.1. The idea is the same, and the tables are different."],[355.0,"An H7 hole is usually checked with a plug gauge:"],[359.0,"the GO end at the lower limit, and the NO-GO end at the upper limit."],[364.0,"A shaft is checked with a ring gauge or a snap gauge, on the same principle."],[370.0,"Measuring with an ordinary caliper at such a grade is hardly enough,"],[374.0,"because a twenty-micron zone is close to the accuracy of the instrument itself."],[379.0,"And in an interference fit, the assembly itself is part of the process:"],[384.0,"pressing, heating the outer part, or cooling the shaft."],[388.0,"So a fit is also read as an indirect instruction to manufacturing:"],[393.0,"which finishing process, which gauge, and which assembly step will be needed."],[401.0,"First, the envelope principle: on an ISO drawing, a fit without an E modifier does"],[407.0,"not guarantee that a bent part will go in, as we saw in the previous chapter."],[413.0,"Second, temperature: the dimensions apply at twenty degrees."],[418.0,"A shaft measured right after machining, while it is warm, measures too large."],[425.0,"machining, you need to know whether the fit applies before"],[433.0,"side can turn a clearance fit into an interference fit."],[441.0,"And fourth, units: in the ISO 286 tables the deviations are written in microns,"],[447.0,"and on the drawing in millimeters. A conversion mistake is a mistake of a thousand times."],[456.0,"A fit designation is a letter and a number:"],[458.0,"the letter sets the position, and the number the width of the zone."],[464.0,"H means a hole that starts exactly at the nominal size."],[472.0,"In the hole-basis system, the shaft's letter immediately tells"],[476.0,"you whether the fit is clearance, transition or interference."],[480.0,"Maximum clearance is the largest hole minus the smallest shaft,"],[485.0,"and minimum clearance is the smallest hole minus the largest shaft."],[491.0,"And for every fit, also check the envelope, the temperature and the coating."],[497.0,"In the next chapter we will combine several dimensions: tolerance stack-ups,"],[503.0,"worst case and RSS, and how to identify on the drawing the dimension that really matters."],[509.0,"See you in chapter eight."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch08"]={"t":"Tolerance Stack-ups","l":[[1.0,"its own. But in the assembly, the parts meet each other."],[8.0,"Several dimensions, each with its own tolerance, accumulate together,"],[13.0,"and create a clearance, an interference or a location that none of them defines alone."],[20.0,"This is a tolerance stack-up, and this is where it is decided whether the assembly will work."],[26.0,"In this chapter we will learn to build a stack-up, calculate it in two ways,"],[30.0,"and identify on the drawing the dimensions that really matter."],[36.0,"dimensions that starts and ends at the same place."],[42.0,"At the center of the loop there is a single requirement: a gap,"],[46.0,"a thickness, or a distance that must stay within certain limits."],[51.0,"This requirement is called the closing dimension."],[54.0,"It is not dimensioned directly, and it is the result of all the others."],[60.0,"Every dimension in the loop gets a direction: a dimension that increases"],[64.0,"the requirement is positive, and a dimension that decreases it is negative."],[70.0,"Dimensions that are not in the loop do not affect the requirement, even if they are very tight."],[76.0,"So the first step is always to draw the loop, not to calculate."],[82.0,"Let's build an example: a bearing housing with a bore fifty deep,"],[86.0,"with a tolerance of plus or minus one tenth."],[90.0,"Inside it sit a bearing twenty wide, a spacer twenty-five long,"],[94.0,"and a washer four and a half thick, each with a tolerance of plus or minus five hundredths."],[101.0,"The requirement is an axial clearance between the washer and the cover,"],[106.0,"which must be between two tenths and eight tenths."],[109.0,"The depth of the bore increases the clearance, so it is positive."],[114.0,"The bearing, the spacer and the washer fill the bore, so they are negative."],[118.0,"minus twenty-five, minus four and a half: half a millimeter."],[127.0,"Half a millimeter is exactly in the middle of the requirement."],[131.0,"But the question is what happens when each dimension moves within its tolerance."],[137.0,"the dimensions are at their worst limit at the same time."],[144.0,"The tolerance of the clearance is simply the sum of all the tolerances."],[149.0,"One tenth, plus three times five hundredths, is twenty-five hundredths."],[155.0,"So the clearance can range from twenty-five hundredths to seventy-five hundredths."],[160.0,"Both results are within the requirement, so the assembly"],[164.0,"will always work, with any combination of conforming parts."],[169.0,"This is the safe method, and it is suitable when there are few parts,"],[174.0,"or when failure is unacceptable in any case."],[178.0,"The second method is RSS, root sum of squares."],[182.0,"It is based on a statistical fact: the chance that all the"],[186.0,"parts reach their worst limit at the same time is almost zero."],[190.0,"Instead of adding the tolerances, you add their squares, and take the square root."],[196.0,"In our example: the square root of one tenth squared,"],[199.0,"plus three times five hundredths squared, comes out to about thirteen hundredths."],[205.0,"That means the expected clearance is between thirty-seven hundredths"],[209.0,"and sixty-three hundredths, much narrower than the worst-case result."],[214.0,"The more parts there are in the stack-up, the greater the advantage of RSS."],[220.0,"But this method has a price: it assumes that every dimension is distributed around the middle,"],[225.0,"and that the processes are stable and independent of each other."],[230.0,"lf a manufacturer systematically aims for the edge of the tolerance,"],[234.0,"the assumption breaks down, and RSS gives an overly optimistic picture."],[240.0,"RSS suits series production, with many parts and processes under statistical control."],[247.0,"When a designer relies on RSS, ASME allows the tolerance"],[250.0,"to be marked with the ST symbol, statistical tolerance."],[255.0,"Next to the symbol there is usually a note that requires statistical process control in production."],[262.0,"Someone who sees ST on a drawing needs to know: meeting the tolerance alone is not enough."],[268.0,"The distribution of the process is also part of the requirement."],[273.0,"And for a single part, or a small batch, RSS is a gamble, and worst case is the right choice."],[282.0,"It is not only dimensions that enter the stack-up."],[286.0,"Geometric tolerances too, such as position, perpendicularity or runout,"],[291.0,"move surfaces, so they are links in the loop."],[294.0,"A position tolerance of one tenth diameter, for example,"],[298.0,"contributes plus or minus five hundredths to the stack-up."],[303.0,"Someone who calculates a stack-up from the dimensions only,"],[307.0,"and skips the geometric tolerances, gets a result better than reality."],[313.0,"Here we return to chapter four: the way a part is dimensioned"],[316.0,"determines how many links it brings into the stack-up."],[320.0,"In a good design, each part contributes only one dimension to the stack-up,"],[324.0,"the dimension that directly touches the requirement."],[328.0,"If the part is dimensioned in an internal chain,"],[331.0,"two or three of its dimensions enter the loop, and the total tolerance grows."],[337.0,"So when you see a direct dimension between two surfaces that are both part of the loop,"],[342.0,"it is usually not a coincidence. It is the critical dimension."],[347.0,"And a manufacturer who converts such dimensions on"],[350.0,"his own changes the stack-up the designer calculated."],[354.0,"What do you do when the calculation shows the requirement is not met?"],[359.0,"You can tighten tolerances, but that makes every part in the stack-up more expensive."],[365.0,"You can add an adjustment component, such as a shim or a"],[369.0,"washer of selected thickness, that closes the gap at assembly."],[374.0,"And you can use selective assembly: sort parts by size, and assemble matching parts together."],[381.0,"Any such solution must appear in the documentation,"],[384.0,"because whoever assembles needs to know it is part of the process."],[390.0,"dimensions around a single requirement, the closing dimension."],[397.0,"Worst case adds all the tolerances, and guarantees assembly with any combination."],[403.0,"and requires stable processes, so it is marked with ST."],[412.0,"Geometric tolerances are part of the stack-up,"],[415.0,"and the direct dimension on the drawing is usually the critical dimension."],[420.0,"This completes the second part of the course. The next chapter starts the third part:"],[426.0,"the datum reference frame. See you in chapter nine."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch09"]={"t":"The Datum Reference Frame","l":[[1.0,"In the third part of the course we will read geometric"],[4.0,"tolerances within the drawing, not each symbol on its own."],[8.0,"And we start from the datum reference frame,"],[11.0,"because every location or orientation tolerance is measured relative to it."],[16.0,"The datum reference frame is the instruction for how to hold the part during inspection,"],[22.0,"and it is also the designer's statement of how the part sits in the assembly."],[27.0,"In this chapter we will learn to read it: what each letter says,"],[31.0,"why the order matters, and what it reveals about the function."],[37.0,"A part floating in space can move in six ways:"],[40.0,"three translations along the axes, and three rotations about them."],[45.0,"These are the six degrees of freedom of the part."],[49.0,"To measure the location of a hole, the part must first be locked,"],[53.0,"so that none of these degrees changes during measurement."],[57.0,"The datum reference frame does exactly that:"],[60.0,"it defines which features of the part lock it, and in which order."],[65.0,"Without a datum reference frame, a position tolerance is a number with no starting point."],[71.0,"So a feature control frame with no datum letters limits only form, not location."],[78.0,"The first letter in the frame is the primary datum."],[81.0,"The part rests on it, touching at least three points."],[85.0,"This locks three degrees of freedom: one translation and two rotations."],[90.0,"The second letter is the secondary datum. The part is pushed against"],[95.0,"it at a minimum of two points, and two more degrees are locked."],[100.0,"The third letter is the tertiary datum. One point is enough, and it locks the last degree."],[107.0,"Three, two, one: this is the principle on which every datum reference frame is built."],[114.0,"In inspection it looks like this: the part is placed on the surface plate,"],[119.0,"pushed against an angle plate, and pressed against a stop."],[123.0,"And the measurement itself is taken from the coordinate"],[126.0,"system that was created, not from the edges of the part."],[131.0,"The order of the letters is not a matter of convenience."],[135.0,"If the primary datum is A, the part sits fully on A, and only then is pushed against B."],[142.0,"If the primary datum is B, the part sits on B, and surface A aligns only as far as it can."],[149.0,"When the angle between the two surfaces is not exactly ninety degrees,"],[154.0,"the two orders give different coordinate systems, and therefore different results."],[160.0,"A hole can pass inspection in one order, and fail in the other."],[164.0,"So the letters are read from left to right,"],[167.0,"exactly in the order they are written, and the order is not changed."],[172.0,"There are three concepts worth separating."],[175.0,"The datum feature is the real surface on the part,"],[179.0,"with all its irregularities. The triangle with the letter is attached to it."],[185.0,"The datum is a theoretically perfect plane, or a perfect axis, from which measurement is taken."],[192.0,"And between them stands the datum feature simulator: the surface plate,"],[197.0,"the angle plate or the precision pin, which actually contact the part."],[202.0,"Measurement is taken from the simulator, because it is what"],[206.0,"the part really meets, both in inspection and in assembly."],[210.0,"So a rough or unstable surface chosen as the primary datum"],[214.0,"will give results that change from one inspection to the next."],[220.0,"A good datum reference frame reflects the assembly."],[223.0,"The primary datum is usually the surface the part sits"],[227.0,"on in the assembly, the largest and most stable surface."],[231.0,"The secondary and tertiary datums are usually the features that locate the part:"],[238.0,"a central bore, a pin, or a wall it is pushed against."],[243.0,"When you read a drawing, ask: are the datum features that"],[246.0,"were chosen the features that really contact the other parts?"],[250.0,"If so, inspection will simulate the assembly. If not,"],[254.0,"a part that passes inspection may not fit in the assembly."],[258.0,"And when the datum reference frame does not look functional,"],[262.0,"that is a reason to ask the designer, not to change it."],[267.0,"On a cast or forged part, the raw surfaces are not true enough to serve as a full datum surface."],[275.0,"So datum targets are used: points, lines or small areas defined on the surface."],[281.0,"They are shown in a circle divided in two: the size of the area on top,"],[287.0,"and the letter and number below, for example A1."],[292.0,"Three targets define the primary datum, two the secondary, and one the tertiary, exactly as before."],[300.0,"That way both the first raw part and the machined part are inspected from"],[305.0,"the same targets, and the transition between the processes is preserved."],[311.0,"A datum feature can also be a feature of size, such as a hole or a shaft."],[317.0,"Then the simulator is a pin or a ring, and the datum is their axis."],[322.0,"When there is no modifier next to the letter in the frame,"],[326.0,"the simulator adjusts itself to the actual size of the hole, and there is no play."],[332.0,"When the letter is followed by M in a circle,"],[335.0,"the simulator is a pin of fixed size, at the maximum material boundary."],[340.0,"A hole larger than that boundary leaves play, and the part can move"],[344.0,"slightly on the pin. This is called datum shift, and it is allowed."],[350.0,"That is exactly what happens in an assembly with a pin,"],[353.0,"so it also makes manufacturing easier without harming the function."],[358.0,"When you read a feature control frame, you can reconstruct the entire inspection from it."],[364.0,"The letters say what the part is placed on and in which order,"],[367.0,"and the modifiers say whether there is play."],[370.0,"A common trap is to inspect all the dimensions from the same frame,"],[374.0,"when different feature control frames on the drawing refer to different datum reference frames."],[381.0,"Another trap is to fixture the part for manufacturing"],[384.0,"on other features, and then be surprised at inspection."],[388.0,"You may manufacture in any fixture, but someone who fixtures the part according"],[393.0,"to the drawing's datum reference frame reduces the risk from the start."],[399.0,"The datum reference frame locks the six degrees of freedom, by the three-two-one method."],[405.0,"The order of the letters determines the result, and it is read exactly as written."],[411.0,"Measurement is taken from the simulator, so a primary datum must be stable and functional."],[417.0,"On raw parts datum targets are used, and a letter with M in a circle allows datum shift."],[424.0,"In the next chapter we will combine geometric tolerances with dimensions,"],[428.0,"and find contradictions on a complete drawing. See you in chapter ten."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch10"]={"t":"Geometric Tolerances and Dimensions Together","l":[[1.0,"In the previous chapter we read the datum reference frame. Now we will read"],[6.0,"how geometric tolerances and dimensions work together, on the same feature."],[11.0,"A single feature can have a size, a form tolerance, an orientation tolerance"],[17.0,"and a location tolerance, and they must all be consistent with each other."],[23.0,"When they are not consistent, the drawing contradicts itself, and there is no way to inspect it."],[30.0,"In this chapter we will learn to spot such contradictions,"],[33.0,"read a material condition bonus, and understand tolerances on hole patterns."],[39.0,"Geometric tolerances have a natural hierarchy:"],[43.0,"location also controls orientation and form, and orientation also controls form."],[49.0,"So a position tolerance on a hole already limits its perpendicularity,"],[54.0,"and a parallelism tolerance on a surface already limits its flatness."],[59.0,"An additional tolerance under a wider one is called a refinement. It makes sense only if it is smaller."],[66.0,"For example, parallelism of two tenths and flatness of five hundredths on the same surface:"],[73.0,"that makes sense. The flatness is refined within the parallelism."],[79.0,"But flatness of two tenths under parallelism of five hundredths adds nothing,"],[84.0,"and that is a sign that something was not thought through."],[88.0,"And the same applies to size: under the envelope principle,"],[92.0,"a flatness larger than the thickness tolerance of the same part is meaningless."],[98.0,"The most common contradiction: a hole located both by"],[101.0,"a plus-or-minus dimension and by a position tolerance."],[106.0,"and there is no way to know which one to inspect to."],[112.0,"When there is a position tolerance, the dimensions that"],[115.0,"locate the hole must be basic dimensions, ina frame."],[119.0,"Another contradiction is an orientation or location tolerance that"],[123.0,"refers to a datum that is not identified on the drawing at all."],[128.0,"And one more: a tolerance that refers to the same feature it applies to,"],[132.0,"as if the hole were measured relative to itself."],[136.0,"In all these cases you do not choose an interpretation."],[139.0,"You record the contradiction and ask the designer."],[143.0,"When the position tolerance is followed by Mina circle, the tolerance applies only"],[149.0,"when the hole is at maximum material condition, that is, at its smallest diameter."],[155.0,"The larger the actual hole, the more it is added to the tolerance,"],[160.0,"by the size of the difference. That is the bonus."],[164.0,"Take a hole with a diameter of ten and a tolerance of plus one tenth,"],[169.0,"with a position tolerance of one tenth and Mina circle."],[173.0,"A hole exactly ten in diameter gets a position tolerance"],[177.0,"of one tenth. A hole 10.1 in diameter gets two tenths."],[181.0,"The reason is functional: a larger hole leaves more room for the bolt,"],[186.0,"so it can be less accurately located and still fit."],[190.0,"The worst case is called the virtual condition:"],[193.0,"the smallest hole diameter minus the position tolerance. Here it is 9.9."],[199.0,"And that is exactly the diameter of the pin on the functional gauge."],[204.0,"If the pin goes into all the holes, the part is good."],[208.0,"L in a circle works the same way, but in reverse: the tolerance applies"],[213.0,"at least material, and the bonus grows as there is more material."],[218.0,"It is used when the concern is a thin wall, not assembly."],[222.0,"A hole close to an edge, for example, may leave a weak wall."],[227.0,"L in a circle guarantees a minimum wall thickness."],[232.0,"the same for every hole size, and there is no bonus."],[239.0,"And the choice between the three options tells the reader what the"],[243.0,"designer was trying to protect: assembly, strength, or precise location."],[249.0,"Sometimes you see a position tolerance of zero, with M in a circle."],[254.0,"That does not mean the hole must be in a perfect location."],[258.0,"It means all the tolerance was moved to size: a hole at maximum material condition"],[265.0,"must be exactly in place, and every increase in diameter is position tolerance."],[271.0,"That way the manufacturer decides for himself how to split the"],[275.0,"tolerance between size and location, and the function is preserved."],[280.0,"One frame can apply to several holes together, for example four holes,"],[285.0,"with the callout four times before the diameter."],[288.0,"Then the holes are inspected as a pattern:"],[291.0,"both the location of each one, and the distances between them."],[295.0,"Sometimes the frame is built of two rows, with one shared"],[299.0,"position symbol. This is a composite position tolerance."],[303.0,"The upper row, the wider one, locates the whole pattern relative to the datums."],[309.0,"The lower row, the tighter one, controls only the distances between"],[313.0,"the holes and their orientation, not the location of the pattern."],[318.0,"In other words: the pattern can move as a block within the wide tolerance,"],[323.0,"but the holes must hold their relationship to each other within the tight tolerance."],[330.0,"This suits a flange that connects to another part with several bolts:"],[334.0,"it is more important for the holes to match each other than for the pattern to be in an exact place."],[341.0,"In ASME, when several patterns refer to the same datums, in the same order"],[346.0,"and with the same modifiers, they are inspected as one pattern, all at once."],[352.0,"To separate them, SEP REQT is written, separate requirement."],[356.0,"In ISO standards the default is the opposite: each pattern is inspected on its own,"],[363.0,"and to combine them you must write SIM, or CZ for a combined zone."],[368.0,"So exactly the same drawing can require one inspection or several, depending on the standard."],[375.0,"There are a few more differences worth knowing when you read"],[378.0,"a drawing to a standard other than the one you are used to."],[381.0,"In the 2018 edition of ASME, the concentricity and symmetry symbols were removed."],[388.0,"Position, profile or runout tolerances are used instead."],[392.0,"In ISO standards both symbols still exist, and you will also find them on older ASME drawings."],[399.0,"And in both standards, a cylindrical tolerance zone requires a diameter symbol before the value."],[406.0,"planes, and that is a completely different requirement."],[414.0,"To avoid missing anything, read every feature in the same order."],[419.0,"First size: what is the dimension, and does the envelope principle apply."],[424.0,"Then form, orientation and location, and check that each one is smaller than the one above it."],[430.0,"Then the datums: do they exist, and are they in the right order."],[434.0,"And finally the modifiers: M, L or nothing, and whether there is a bonus or a datum shift."],[441.0,"If anything in this chain does not add up, itis a question"],[445.0,"for the designer, not a decision for whoever is measuring."],[450.0,"Geometric tolerances and dimensions must be consistent: a refinement is smaller than"],[456.0,"the tolerance above it, and a position tolerance is located only by basic dimensions."],[462.0,"M in a circle gives a bonus for assembly, L ina circle protects a wall, and on hole patterns a"],[469.0,"composite tolerance separates the location of the pattern from the relationship between the holes."],[476.0,"In the next chapter we start the fourth part: surface texture,"],[481.0,"and what Ra says and what it does not say. See you in chapter eleven."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch11"]={"t":"Surface Texture","l":[[1.0,"Two surfaces can be exactly the same size, and still behave completely differently."],[7.0,"One seals, and the other leaks. One wears slowly, and the other fails from fatigue."],[15.0,"The difference lies in the surface texture: the small hills and valleys the tool leaves behind."],[22.0,"In this chapter we start the fourth part of the course,"],[26.0,"and learn to read surface texture symbols, and what Ra says and what it does not say."],[34.0,"When you magnify a section of a machined surface, you see three layers of deviation."],[40.0,"The overall form, such as a curve or a slope, is controlled by geometric tolerances."],[47.0,"Waviness is a long-wavelength deviation, usually caused by vibration or by deflection of the tool."],[55.0,"And roughness is the short wavelength, the marks of the tool itself."],[59.0,"Most of the symbol on the drawing deals with it."],[63.0,"The tool marks also run in a certain direction, and that is called the lay."],[69.0,"The most common parameter is Ra: the average of the"],[73.0,"deviations from the mean line of the profile, in microns."],[77.0,"Ra is a stable number and convenient to measure, so it appears on most drawings."],[84.0,"But it has a weakness: two completely different surfaces can have the same Ra."],[90.0,"A surface with sharp peaks and a surface with narrow valleys will give the same average,"],[96.0,"even though they behave in opposite ways in sealing and in wear."],[100.0,"And a single scratch hardly changes Ra, because it is swallowed by the average."],[107.0,"Rz measures the height between the highest peak and the deepest"],[111.0,"valley in each sampling length, and calculates the average."],[116.0,"So Rz is much more sensitive to individual peaks and scratches."],[121.0,"There is no fixed ratio between Ra and Rz. The ratio depends on the process,"],[127.0,"so you Cannot convert between them with a formula."],[132.0,"Someone who replaces Rz with Ra using a conversion"],[135.0,"table changes the designer's requirement."],[139.0,"And on inch drawings the value is written in micro- inches."],[143.0,"An Ra of thirty-two micro-inches is about eight tenths of a micron."],[150.0,"The basic symbol is a broken line, like a V with one long leg, touching the surface."],[156.0,"removal is required, that is, the surface must be machined."],[164.0,"A circle inside the V says the opposite: material removal is prohibited."],[171.0,"The surface stays as it is after casting, forging or pressing."],[177.0,"process is allowed, as long as the texture is achieved."],[185.0,"The parameter and the value are written on the long leg, for example Ra eight tenths."],[191.0,"A mandatory manufacturing process, a machining allowance, and a lay symbol can also be added."],[199.0,"run relative to the line on which the symbol is drawn."],[206.0,"The equals sign means parallel, the perpendicular sign means perpendicular,"],[212.0,"X means crossed, C means circular, and M means multidirectional."],[218.0,"The lay is especially important on sliding surfaces and seals,"],[222.0,"because it determines whether fluid will flow along the marks or be stopped by them."],[228.0,"And the measurement depends on it too: Ra is usually measured perpendicular to the lay."],[235.0,"Measuring parallel to it gives a number that is too low."],[240.0,"In ISO, the symbol is now defined in ISO 21920, which replaced ISO 1302 in 2021."],[251.0,"In ASME, the symbol is defined in ASME Y14.36, and the form is very similar."],[259.0,"On older ASME drawings, a number written next to the symbol without a parameter name is Ra."],[266.0,"And in ISO there is an important rule: by default,"],[270.0,"up to sixteen percent of the measurements may exceed the value."],[277.0,"When max is written after the parameter, no measurement may exceed it."],[282.0,"And the cutoff length is set by the size of the value. For Ra between"],[287.0,"one tenth and two microns, the cutoff is eight tenths of a millimeter."],[294.0,"On most drawings, not every surface is marked separately."],[298.0,"Next to the title block a general symbol appears,"],[302.0,"for example Ra 3.2, followed by a symbol in parentheses."],[307.0,"That means: all surfaces 3.2, except those marked otherwise on the drawing."],[313.0,"So when a particular surface is not marked, it is still not free. It takes the general symbol."],[321.0,"Every process has a natural range of roughness."],[324.0,"Ordinary turning and milling usually give between eight tenths and six microns."],[330.0,"Reaming gives about four tenths to three microns."],[334.0,"Grinding usually reaches one tenth to one and a half microns,"],[338.0,"and polishing or lapping less than half a micron."],[342.0,"So the roughness value on the drawing is also an indirect instruction"],[347.0,"for the process. An Ra of two tenths almost always requires grinding."],[353.0,"And every step toward lower roughness adds an operation, time and cost."],[359.0,"There are a few traps worth knowing."],[361.0,"On a coated part, you need to know whether the roughness applies"],[365.0,"before or after coating, because coating can change it considerably."],[369.0,"On a shaft running against a seal, Ra alone is not enough."],[373.0,"Turning leaves helical marks that can pump fluid out."],[378.0,"And a single deep scratch can cause a leak or a crack, and still not change Ra."],[384.0,"When the function is sensitive to this, the designer needs to add a parameter such as Rz,"],[391.0,"or an explicit requirement. And if it is missing, you ask."],[396.0,"Surface texture is roughness, waviness and lay, and each of them affects the function."],[402.0,"Ra is a stable average, but blind to peaks and scratches."],[406.0,"Rz is more sensitive to them, and there is no fixed conversion between them."],[412.0,"The symbol says whether material removal is required, prohibited or allowed,"],[417.0,"and the general symbol appears in parentheses next to the title block."],[422.0,"In the next chapter we will get to know welding symbols,"],[426.0,"and the differences between the American standard and ISO. See you in chapter twelve."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch12"]={"t":"Welding Symbols","l":[[1.0,"The welding symbol is one of the densest symbols on a drawing."],[6.0,"One small line encodes the weld type, size, length, side and process."],[11.0,"It is also one of the easiest symbols to read backwards,"],[15.0,"because there are two main standards, and they do not read the side the same way."],[21.0,"Between the American standard, AWS A2.4, and the international standard,"],[28.0,"ISO 2553, there is also a difference in the meaning of the numbers."],[35.0,"In this chapter we will take the symbol apart, and see exactly where the traps are."],[41.0,"The basis is a horizontal reference line, with an arrow running from it to the joint."],[47.0,"The weld symbol is drawn on the reference line, with the dimensions next to it."],[52.0,"At the other end of the line there can be a tail, a small fork."],[57.0,"The process or the specification is written inside it."],[61.0,"And where the arrow meets the line there is room for two special symbols: a circle,"],[67.0,"meaning weld all around, and a flag, meaning field weld, done during assembly."],[74.0,"A triangle is a fillet weld, the most common."],[79.0,"A V-shaped symbol is a groove weld with two bevels."],[82.0,"A straight line and half a V are a groove with a single bevel."],[87.0,"Two parallel lines are a square-groove weld, with no edge preparation."],[93.0,"There are also symbols for U and J grooves, plug welds, spot welds and seam welds."],[100.0,"And additional marks above the symbol set the contour of the weld face: flush, convex or concave,"],[107.0,"and sometimes a letter that says how to finish it, for example G for grinding."],[115.0,"Here lies the big trap. The arrow points to a joint, and the joint has two sides."],[121.0,"In the American standard: a symbol drawn below the reference line is on the"],[126.0,"side the arrow points to. A symbol above the line is on the other side."],[131.0,"A symbol on both sides of the line means welding on both sides."],[136.0,"In ISO there are two systems. System A uses a double reference line:"],[142.0,"a solid line and a dashed line beneath it."],[146.0,"In system A, a symbol on the solid line is on the arrow side,"],[150.0,"and a symbol on the dashed line is on the other side."],[155.0,"ISO system B works with a single line, like the American standard."],[160.0,"So when you see a double line, it is an ISO system A drawing,"],[164.0,"and you read by the solid line, not by above and below."],[169.0,"Someone who reads such a drawing with American eyes will weld on the wrong side."],[175.0,"In the American standard, the number to the left of the triangle is the leg size of the fillet weld."],[182.0,"In ISO a letter must appear before the number: a for throat thickness, or z for leg length."],[189.0,"And the difference is large: a5 means a throat thickness"],[193.0,"of five millimeters, and a leg of about seven millimeters."],[198.0,"Someone who welds a five-millimeter leg where a5 is written"],[202.0,"produces a weld about thirty percent weaker than designed."],[207.0,"And a number without a letter on an ISO drawing is a question for the designer, not a guess."],[214.0,"The weld length is written to the right of the symbol."],[217.0,"With no length, the weld runs the full length of the joint."],[222.0,"For intermittent welds, in the American standard, a length and a pitch are written: 50-150,"],[230.0,"meaning segments fifty millimeters long, every hundred and fifty, center to center."],[238.0,"In ISO the notation is different: the number of segments,"],[242.0,"times the length, and in parentheses the spacing between segments."],[248.0,"is the spacing from end to end, not center to center."],[257.0,"Someone who confuses the two gets a different number of segments,"],[260.0,"and a different total weld length."],[264.0,"In a groove weld, the numbers describe the preparation and the penetration."],[269.0,"The number to the left of the symbol is the depth of preparation, and next to it,"],[275.0,"in parentheses, the effective throat, that is, how far the weld penetrates."],[281.0,"The root opening is written inside the symbol, and the groove angle above it."],[286.0,"And when the penetration requirement is missing, it is not clear whether full or"],[291.0,"partial penetration is required, and that is a huge difference in strength and cost."],[298.0,"The welding process is written in the tail, for example TIG or MIG,"],[303.0,"or an ISO process number, such as 141 for TIG."],[308.0,"The tail often refers to a welding procedure specification,"],[312.0,"a WPS, which defines all the parameters."],[316.0,"The symbol itself does not say how many imperfections are allowed."],[322.0,"That is set by a quality standard, such as ISO 5817, with quality levels B, C and D."],[331.0,"And if the drawing does not refer to a quality standard,"],[334.0,"there is no definition for weld acceptance, and you need to ask."],[340.0,"The American standard also has a symbol for nondestructive testing,"],[344.0,"with the same line-and-arrow structure."],[347.0,"Abbreviations such as RT for radiography, UT for ultrasonic,"],[352.0,"MT for magnetic particle, PT for liquid penetrant and VT for visual inspection."],[358.0,"This symbol says what to test and where, not how to decide whether the"],[363.0,"weld is acceptable. That too is set by the standard the drawing refers to."],[370.0,"A summary of the welding symbol traps."],[373.0,"First: the arrow side. First identify whether the drawing"],[378.0,"is American or ISO, and whether there is a double line."],[383.0,"Second: fillet weld size. In ISO, check whether a or Z is written."],[392.0,"Third: intermittent welds. Pitch center to center, versus spacing between segments."],[400.0,"Fourth: the small circle and flag, which are easy to miss, and change the scope of the work."],[406.0,"And fifth: a welding drawing is usually an assembly drawing,"],[410.0,"and every weld refers to two parts that need to be identified."],[417.0,"A welding symbol is made of a reference line, an arrow, a weld symbol, dimensions and a tail."],[424.0,"In the American standard, below the line is the arrow side."],[429.0,"In ISO system A, the solid line is the arrow side."],[433.0,"In ISO, a is the throat thickness and z is the leg length, and a5 equals a leg of about seven."],[442.0,"For intermittent welds, the American pitch is center to center,"],[447.0,"and the number in parentheses in ISO is the spacing between segments."],[454.0,"And quality is set by a separate standard, not by the symbol."],[459.0,"In the next chapter we will read the material,"],[462.0,"heat treatment and coating on the drawing. See you in chapter thirteen."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch13"]={"t":"Material, Heat Treatment and Coating","l":[[1.0,"Two parts can be identical in dimensions,"],[4.0,"and completely different in strength, hardness and durability."],[8.0,"The difference is written on the drawing, but not in the picture: in the title block and in the notes."],[17.0,"The material, its condition, the heat treatment and the coating"],[21.0,"are part of the definition of the part, just like the dimensions."],[25.0,"In this chapter we will learn to read all these definitions, and in which order they apply."],[32.0,"The material usually appears in the title block, in the material field."],[37.0,"It is written to a particular standard: for example 1.4404"],[42.0,"under the European standard, or 316L under the American system."],[47.0,"Often a product standard also appears, which defines the form of"],[52.0,"the material and its properties, for example plate, bar or tube."],[58.0,"In many alloys, the temper is part of the material. Aluminum 6061 in the T6"],[63.0,"temper is very different from the same aluminum in the annealed condition."],[70.0,"And T651 says something more: the material was stretched to relieve stress,"],[75.0,"and that affects distortion during machining."],[79.0,"Someone who substitutes a material that looks equivalent to him, without approval,"],[85.0,"changes the part. An equivalent between standards is close, not identical."],[92.0,"And a material certificate is usually required too, for example to EN 10204 type 3.1,"],[99.0,"proving that the actual material is the material on the drawing."],[106.0,"Heat treatment appears in a note, and defines what the part must"],[110.0,"be after the treatment, not only which treatment to carry out."],[114.0,"example forty to forty-five on the Rockwell C scale."],[121.0,"For case hardening, such as carburizing or nitriding,"],[125.0,"the case depth also appears, and the hardness at which that depth is measured."],[130.0,"When only part of the surface is hardened, the area is marked"],[135.0,"on the drawing with a thick chain line, according to ISO 15787."],[140.0,"And the location where the hardness is tested is usually marked as well,"],[145.0,"because on a large part the hardness is not uniform."],[149.0,"A hardness requirement with no test location is incomplete,"],[153.0,"because every point on the part can give a different result."],[158.0,"There are several hardness scales, and each is measured by a different method."],[163.0,"Rockwell C for hard steels, Brinell for softer materials and castings,"],[168.0,"and Vickers for thin layers and laboratory measurements."],[173.0,"There are conversion tables between the scales, but the conversion is approximate."],[178.0,"So when the drawing requires a particular scale, you measure on that scale."],[183.0,"enough for acceptance, unless otherwise approved."],[191.0,"The coating too is defined by a specification, not just by a name."],[196.0,"Anodizing, for example, can be decorative or hard, dyed or clear,"],[201.0,"and each one has a different thickness and different properties."],[205.0,"So the note usually includes the standard, the type, the class and the thickness."],[212.0,"For zinc plating to ISO 2081, for example, the definition"],[216.0,"includes the coating thickness and the type of post-treatment."],[220.0,"And on stainless steels, a passivation note refers to a standard such as ASTM A967."],[228.0,"This is not a coating, but a chemical treatment of the natural passive layer."],[236.0,"A coating adds material, so it changes dimensions."],[240.0,"A layer of ten microns on each side increases a shaft by"],[244.0,"twenty microns, and reduces a hole by twenty microns."],[248.0,"penetrates into the material, and half builds up on top of it."],[255.0,"So it is important to know whether the dimensions on the drawing apply before or after coating."],[262.0,"This is usually written in a note, and when it is not written, you ask."],[267.0,"Areas that must not be coated, such as threads or fit surfaces,"],[271.0,"are marked on the drawing as areas to be masked during coating."],[276.0,"A coated thread without prior allowance may not go together,"],[279.0,"and a hole with a tight fit may not accept the shaft."],[284.0,"There is one trap that is not just a question of size."],[288.0,"In high-strength steels, electroplating can introduce hydrogen into the material."],[293.0,"The hydrogen causes embrittlement, and the part can"],[297.0,"break without warning, hours or days after assembly."],[301.0,"So the specification usually requires hydrogen embrittlement relief baking, shortly after plating."],[308.0,"When the drawing specifies a hard steel and electroplating,"],[312.0,"and there is no baking requirement, that is an urgent question for the designer."],[319.0,"The notes on the drawing also define, explicitly or implicitly, a sequence of operations."],[326.0,"Usually: material in a given condition, rough machining,"],[330.0,"heat treatment, finish machining, and coating only at the end."],[335.0,"Heat treatment after finish machining can distort the part,"],[339.0,"so a machining allowance is left on the precise surfaces."],[343.0,"And when the note says the dimensions apply after heat treatment,"],[347.0,"the manufacturer has to plan the allowance himself."],[351.0,"Someone who reads the notes as a list and not as a process may carry them out in the wrong order."],[358.0,"marking: what to mark, where, and by which method."],[367.0,"The method matters: engraving or stamping ona highly"],[370.0,"stressed surface can create a crack initiation point."],[374.0,"So the location is usually defined in an area that is neither stressed nor functional."],[382.0,"An equivalent material without approval."],[384.0,"A missing material condition, or one that was misread."],[388.0,"Hardness without a test location, or measurement on a different scale."],[393.0,"Coating that was not taken into account in the tolerance of a fit or a thread."],[399.0,"And a hard steel with electroplating, without baking."],[403.0,"The material and its condition are part of the definition of the part,"],[407.0,"and they are not substituted without approval."],[411.0,"Heat treatment is defined by its result: a hardness range, a case depth and a test location."],[418.0,"A coating is defined by a specification, changes dimensions,"],[422.0,"and requires knowing whether the dimensions are before or after coating."],[428.0,"And all the notes together define a sequence of operations, which must be read as a process."],[435.0,"In the next chapter we go deeper into threads: metric, inch,"],[440.0,"pipe threads and Helicoil. See you in chapter fourteen."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch14"]={"t":"Threads in Depth","l":[[1.0,"In chapter three we learned how a thread is drawn. Now we will learn"],[6.0,"to read its callout, and that is where most of the information is."],[11.0,"A single thread callout gives the diameter, the pitch, the tolerance,"],[16.0,"the depth, and sometimes also the hand and the number of starts."],[21.0,"And when a metric thread is swapped for an inch thread,"],[25.0,"or one pipe thread for another, the screw goes in half a turn and jams."],[30.0,"In this chapter we will cover metric threads, inch threads, pipe threads and Helicoil holes."],[39.0,"A metric callout starts with the letter M and the nominal diameter, for example M10."],[46.0,"When no pitch is written, it is the standard coarse pitch. For M10 it is one and a half millimeters."],[54.0,"A fine pitch is always written explicitly, for example M10x1.25."],[61.0,"After a dash comes the thread tolerance class, and then the depth."],[66.0,"depth, and the tap drill depth, which is always longer."],[75.0,"diameter to a depth of 20, and tap to a depth of 16."],[85.0,"Someone who drills only to the thread depth leaves no room for the tip of the tap,"],[90.0,"and will not get a full thread to the required depth."],[95.0,"A thread tolerance is built like the ISO 286 fits:"],[99.0,"a number for the tolerance grade, and a letter for the position."],[104.0,"An upper-case letter for an internal thread, and a lower-case letter for an external thread."],[111.0,"6H and 6g are the most common combination, and it suits most bolts and nuts."],[118.0,"4h is a more precise thread, and 6e leaves a larger allowance, for example room for plating."],[127.0,"When the part is coated after threading, you must check that the"],[131.0,"tolerance suits the coating thickness, otherwise the bolt will not go in."],[137.0,"And in inspection, the governing dimension is the pitch diameter, not the major diameter."],[145.0,"Inch threads are written differently: diameter, threads per inch, series and class."],[153.0,"1/4-20 UNC-2A, for example: a quarter-inch diameter,"],[158.0,"twenty threads per inch, coarse series, class two, external."],[164.0,"UNF is the fine series, and at the same diameter it has more threads per inch."],[171.0,"A denotes an external thread and B an internal one."],[175.0,"The classes are one, two and three, and three is the most precise."],[181.0,"Small diameters are written as a number, for example 10-32 UNF."],[189.0,"And there is a well-known trap: 1/4-20 and M6 look almost the same,"],[195.0,"and one screw will go into the other a few turns before it jams."],[203.0,"In pipe threads, the number in the callout is not the diameter of the thread."],[208.0,"A half-inch pipe thread, for example, has an outside diameter of about twenty-one millimeters."],[215.0,"NPT is the American thread: tapered, with a sixty- degree angle,"],[220.0,"and the seal is made on the thread itself, with a sealant."],[225.0,"In the British system, G is a parallel thread to ISO 228."],[230.0,"It does not seal by itself, and sealing is done with a gasket or an O-ring."],[237.0,"RIS a tapered external thread, RC a taperea internal one, and Rp a parallel internal one, to ISO Te"],[248.0,"And the thread angle in the British thread is fifty- five degrees,"],[253.0,"so NPT and BSPT are not interchangeable, even though they look similar."],[259.0,"Someone who assembles a G thread with sealant on the thread instead of a gasket will get a leak."],[267.0,"When threading into a soft material, such as aluminum,"],[272.0,"a wire thread insert, a Helicoil, is often used."],[277.0,"The drawing shows the final thread, for example M8,"],[280.0,"and the length of the insert, for example one and a half times the diameter."],[286.0,"But the hole itself is tapped with a special, larger thread called STI, with a special tap."],[295.0,"Someone who taps a regular M8 instead of STI will not be able to install the insert."],[302.0,"So when you see a Helicoil on the drawing, check exactly what"],[306.0,"must be drilled, tapped and inserted, and in which order."],[312.0,"LH after the callout means a left-hand thread. Without LH, the thread is right-hand."],[318.0,"In a multi-start thread, the lead and the pitch are written,"],[324.0,"for example Ph3 P1.5, meaning a two-start thread."],[329.0,"A chamfer is sometimes shown at the start of the thread, so the screw will start easily."],[335.0,"And at the end of an external thread there is sometimes a thread undercut,"],[341.0,"to standards such as DIN 76, so the thread is full up to the shoulder."],[348.0,"Threads are usually checked with gauges: a plug gauge for an"],[352.0,"internal thread, and a ring gauge for an external thread."],[356.0,"Every gauge has a GO end and a NO-GO end."],[360.0,"The GO end must enter the full length of the thread without force."],[365.0,"The NO-GO end is allowed to enter up to about two turns, and no more."],[371.0,"And the gauges must be of the same tolerance class as the drawing: a 6H gauge for a 6H thread."],[380.0,"A metric versus an inch thread at similar diameters."],[384.0,"NPT versus BSPT, and G versus R."],[390.0,"A tap drill hole too short in a blind hole."],[394.0,"A coated thread with a tolerance that leaves no room for the coating."],[398.0,"A Helicoil without an STI tap."],[401.0,"And a left-hand thread that nobody noticed."],[405.0,"In a metric callout: diameter, pitch if it is fine, tolerance and depth."],[412.0,"In an inch callout: diameter, threads per inch, series, class, and A or B."],[419.0,"In pipe threads, the number is a name, not a diameter, and you distinguish"],[424.0,"tapered from parallel, and sealing on the thread from sealing with a gasket."],[430.0,"With a Helicoil, the hole is tapped STI, and the drawing shows the final thread."],[436.0,"And inspection is with GO and NO-GO gauges, of the same tolerance class as the drawing."],[443.0,"In the next chapter we will get to know special features: edge breaks,"],[448.0,"undercuts, splines and tables on the drawing. See you in chapter fifteen."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch15"]={"t":"Special Features","l":[[1.0,"A drawing has small features that take up little space, but define a great deal."],[7.0,"An edge, a small groove at the end of a shaft, a spline, or a table in the corner"],[13.0,"of the sheet can decide whether the part will assemble, hold, and pass inspection."],[20.0,"They are usually defined by reference to a standard,"],[23.0,"not by a full drawing, so you need to know how to read the reference."],[28.0,"undercuts, splines, keyways, center holes, knurling and tables."],[37.0,"A note such as break sharp edges is one of the most common"],[41.0,"notes on a drawing, and also one of the least defined."],[45.0,"How much to break? With a chamfer or a radius? And what is allowed to remain?"],[51.0,"ISO 13715 gives this a precise language: a symbol next to the edge, and a number with a sign."],[60.0,"A positive number means material may remain beyond"],[63.0,"the ideal edge, such as a burr, up to that value."],[68.0,"A negative number means the edge must be removed,"],[71.0,"with a chamfer or a radius, up to that depth."],[75.0,"And plus or minus means either is allowed, within the limit."],[80.0,"A general note to the standard, next to the title block,"],[83.0,"defines every edge not marked otherwise, like a general tolerance."],[89.0,"At the end of a ground shaft, next to the shoulder, a small undercut often appears."],[96.0,"Without it, the grinding wheel or the tool leaves a small radius in the corner,"],[101.0,"and the mating part will not seat flush against the shoulder."],[105.0,"The undercut is usually defined by reference to a standard,"],[111.0,"such as DIN 509, with a form and dimensions, for example E1 by 0.2."],[118.0,"The letter denotes the form of the undercut, and the numbers the radius and the depth."],[124.0,"Someone who replaces the undercut with an ordinary radius changes"],[128.0,"the contact length and the stress concentration in the corner."],[134.0,"A spline is a shaft with teeth that transmits torque to a hub."],[139.0,"representation, and the whole definition is in a data table."],[147.0,"The table lists the number of teeth, the module, the pressure angle,"],[153.0,"the tolerance class and the fit class, to a standard such as ISO 4156 or DIN 5480."],[162.0,"And there is usually an inspection dimension too: measurement over"],[166.0,"pins on an external spline, or between pins on an internal spline."],[172.0,"matters more than any other dimension in the table."],[179.0,"A keyway is a slot for a key, which transmits torque between a shaft and a hub."],[184.0,"The keyway dimensions are usually taken from a standard,"],[188.0,"such as DIN 6885, according to the shaft diameter."],[193.0,"system, such as N9 for a normal fit or P9 for a tight fit."],[201.0,"And the depth is sometimes dimensioned from the opposite side of the shaft,"],[206.0,"not from the surface, so it can be measured directly."],[210.0,"Someone who measures the depth from a different point than the one dimensioned on"],[215.0,"the drawing will get a different number and make the wrong acceptance decision."],[222.0,"On parts machined between centers, the end of the shaft gets a center hole."],[228.0,"It is defined to ISO 6411, with a form and diameters, for example A 2.5 over 5.3."],[237.0,"And the standard also gives three symbols:"],[240.0,"the center hole must remain, may remain, or must not remain."],[245.0,"This matters, because a center hole that remains can be used for"],[250.0,"inspection and repair in the future, or get in the way of the mating part."],[257.0,"Knurling is a pattern rolled onto the surface of a shaft,"],[260.0,"usually so it can be gripped or pressed into another part."],[265.0,"The drawing gives the type of knurl, straight or diamond,"],[269.0,"the pitch, and sometimes a standard such as DIN 82."],[273.0,"Knurling increases the diameter, so when it is used for a press fit,"],[278.0,"the diameter after knurling is the important dimension."],[283.0,"Tables on a drawing are a way to gather a lot of information in one place."],[289.0,"A tabulated drawing, for example, defines several similar parts on one drawing:"],[295.0,"each row is a part number, and each column is a dimension with a letter."],[301.0,"The picture carries the letters, and the table the values for each part number."],[306.0,"the part being made, and only then read dimensions."],[314.0,"A mistake in the row is a mistake in every dimension at once."],[319.0,"A hole table, a bend table for sheet metal, or a spline data"],[324.0,"table is read the same way: identify the row, then the column."],[330.0,"A summary of the traps in special features."],[334.0,"Break sharp edges with no value is an undefined requirement."],[338.0,"A reference to the standard, with a number and a sign, is better."],[343.0,"An undercut replaced with a radius changes how the part seats on the shoulder."],[349.0,"On a spline, the inspection dimension over or between pins governs."],[355.0,"On a keyway, check from which point the depth is dimensioned."],[360.0,"And in tables, identify the right row first."],[364.0,"Small features are usually defined by reference to a standard, not by the picture."],[371.0,"Edges to ISO 13715: positive allows material, negative requires removal."],[380.0,"Undercuts to DIN 509, keyways to DIN 6885, and center holes to ISO 6411."],[389.0,"Splines in a data table, with an inspection dimension over or between pins."],[395.0,"And in every table, identify the row, then the column."],[400.0,"In the next chapter the fifth part begins: the drawing as a document."],[406.0,"We start with the title block and the notes. See you in chapter sixteen."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch16"]={"t":"The Title Block and the Notes","l":[[1.0,"The fifth part of the course deals with the drawing as a document:"],[5.0,"not only what is drawn, but everything around it."],[9.0,"And it starts with the title block, in the bottom right corner of the sheet."],[14.0,"Most readers look at it only to find the number. But it defines how to read everything else."],[20.0,"In this chapter we will go through the fields of the title block,"],[24.0,"the notes, and the documents the drawing refers to."],[28.0,"ISO 7200 defines the basic fields: title, identification number,"],[34.0,"revision, date, sheet number, and the legal owner of the document."],[41.0,"On drawings to ASME, the sheet size, the scale,"],[45.0,"a CAGE code and the general tolerance block usually appear as well."],[50.0,"The drawing number and the part number are not always the same number."],[55.0,"One drawing can define several parts, as we saw in a tabulated drawing."],[60.0,"And the sheet number, for example one of three,"],[64.0,"says there are more sheets, and each of them is part of the definition."],[69.0,"Someone who works from one sheet only may miss a dimension,"],[74.0,"a section or a note that is on another sheet."],[79.0,"The revision is one of the most important fields."],[82.0,"It says which version of the drawing is in your hands, and every change to the part raises it."],[88.0,"So before starting work, check that the revision on the drawing"],[93.0,"matches the revision on the purchase order or the work order."],[98.0,"And on a multi-sheet drawing, check that all the sheets in your hands are at the same revision."],[104.0,"A drawing at an old revision is one of the most common sources of"],[108.0,"rejected parts, even though every dimension was made correctly."],[114.0,"The scale is written in the title block, for example one to two."],[118.0,"But as we saw in chapter zero, you do not measure from the picture."],[123.0,"The scale helps you understand the size, and is not used for measurement."],[128.0,"When a particular view is drawn at a different scale,"],[132.0,"such as an enlarged detail, its scale is written next to it."],[138.0,"The title block also defines the reading rules for the whole drawing."],[143.0,"The projection symbol says whether the views are in first angle or third angle."],[148.0,"The reference to the tolerancing standard, such as ASME Y14.5 or ISO 8015,"],[154.0,"says whether the envelope principle or the independency principle applies."],[160.0,"And the units, millimeters or inches, are written there. Someone who assumes the units"],[167.0,"without checking may make a part twenty-five times too large, or too small by the same ratio."],[174.0,"And the general tolerances, the default surface texture and"],[178.0,"the general edge break usually appear in this area too."],[183.0,"The general notes appear next to the title block. They apply to the whole drawing."],[189.0,"There are also flag notes: a number inside a triangle or a flag,"],[193.0,"which appears next to a particular feature in the picture."],[198.0,"notes list, and it applies only where the flag appears."],[205.0,"So when you read a numbered note, look in the picture for"],[208.0,"every flag with the same number, not just the first one."],[213.0,"What happens when notes contradict each other?"],[215.0,"Usually, a specific requirement next to a feature overrides a"],[220.0,"general note. That is what unless otherwise specified means."],[225.0,"But when the contradiction is between two specific requirements,"],[230.0,"or between the drawing and a specification, there is no automatic rule."],[236.0,"In that case you do not choose the convenient interpretation."],[240.0,"You record the contradiction, and you ask."],[244.0,"A drawing almost always refers to other documents: a material specification,"],[250.0,"a process specification, an inspection standard, or customer requirements."],[257.0,"These documents are part of the drawing in every respect."],[260.0,"Their requirements are binding as if they were written on the sheet."],[265.0,"And they have a revision too. When the drawing does not specify a revision of"],[270.0,"a specification, the current revision usually applies, and that can change."],[276.0,"So someone working from a specification saved on a computer"],[280.0,"a few years ago may be working to outdated requirements."],[285.0,"The title block also carries the approval fields: drawn, checked and approved."],[292.0,"A drawing without approval is a draft, even if it looks finished."],[297.0,"And in some organizations the document status appears too,"],[301.0,"for example for reference only, or released for production."],[305.0,"Manufacturing from a drawing that has not been released"],[308.0,"is working to a requirement that can still change."],[312.0,"This gives a simple reading order."],[315.0,"First the title block: number, revision, sheets, units, standard and projection symbol."],[324.0,"Then the general notes and the flag notes, and the documents the drawing refers to."],[330.0,"And only then the views and the dimensions."],[333.0,"them without knowing the rules they were written by."],[340.0,"The title block defines the identity of the drawing and its reading rules."],[345.0,"The revision and the sheets determine exactly what is in your hands."],[350.0,"Flag notes apply only where the flag appears, and a specific requirement overrides a general one."],[357.0,"Referenced documents are part of the drawing, and they have a revision too."],[362.0,"And in the next chapter we will read assembly drawings: parts lists,"],[368.0,"balloons and tightening torques. See you in chapter seventeen."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch17"]={"t":"Assembly Drawings","l":[[1.0,"A part drawing says what one part looks like."],[4.0,"An assembly drawing says how the parts come together."],[8.0,"It does not redefine the geometry of each part. That is the job of the part drawings."],[15.0,"But it defines what is assembled, how many of each, in which order,"],[19.0,"with what force, and what must be achieved at the end."],[24.0,"So misreading an assembly drawing can reject a whole assembly,"],[28.0,"even when every part on its own is conforming."],[32.0,"In this chapter we will read the parts list, the balloons,"],[36.0,"the assembly notes, and the dimensions that appear only in the assembly."],[42.0,"An assembly drawing usually includes a view or a section showing all the parts in place."],[49.0,"In an assembly section, as we saw in chapter two, shafts,"],[53.0,"bolts and pins are not hatched, and adjacent parts get hatching at different angles."],[60.0,"That way you can tell the boundary between two parts,"],[63.0,"even when they are made of the same material."],[66.0,"There are few dimensions on an assembly drawing, and they belong to the assembly only:"],[73.0,"overall dimensions, locations, or dimensions to be achieved after assembly."],[80.0,"The parts list is the table that defines what goes into the assembly."],[84.0,"It is defined in standards such as ISO 7573 and ASME Y14.34, and it usually has an item number,"],[94.0,"a part number, a description, a quantity, and a material or standard."],[101.0,"The item number is the number that appears in the balloon,"],[104.0,"and the part number is the number of the drawing or of the purchased part."],[109.0,"Standard parts are described by their standard,"],[115.0,"for example an ISO 4762 screw, M8x25, property class 12.9."],[122.0,"And for consumables, such as adhesive, sealant or shims,"],[127.0,"the quantity is sometimes written as AR, as required."],[132.0,"Someone who replaces a standard screw with one that looks"],[135.0,"identical but has a different property class changes the assembly."],[141.0,"A balloon is a circle with an item number, and a leader line pointing to the part in the picture."],[148.0,"When the leader ends in a dot, it points to the surface of the part."],[152.0,"When it ends in an arrowhead, it points to its outline."],[157.0,"Every item in the list must appear in a balloon at least once."],[161.0,"Identical parts that repeat several times get one balloon, and the quantity is written in the list."],[169.0,"And for fastener sets, such as a screw, a washer and a nut,"],[173.0,"a stack of balloons on a single leader is sometimes used."],[177.0,"When there is a balloon with no row in the list, or a row with no balloon,"],[182.0,"that is an error on the drawing, and you need to ask."],[188.0,"Most of the assembly information is in the notes."],[191.0,"A tightening torque, for example twenty-five newton meters,"],[196.0,"appears next to the screw or in a general note."],[200.0,"And it must always come with a condition: whether the thread is dry or lubricated."],[205.0,"The same torque on a lubricated thread produces a much higher clamping force."],[211.0,"In a joint with several screws, a tightening sequence sometimes appears too,"],[217.0,"for example in a cross pattern, and in several steps."],[221.0,"Other notes define a threadlocker, lubrication, or a sealant, to a specification."],[227.0,"Someone who tightens without reading the condition may tighten"],[231.0,"less than required, or stretch the screw until it fails."],[236.0,"The assembly drawing often shows the fits between the parts,"],[241.0,"for example H7/g6 next to a shaft in a bushing."],[246.0,"For an interference fit, the note also defines the assembly method:"],[251.0,"pressing, heating the outer part, or cooling the shaft."],[256.0,"And when the assembly includes an adjustment, such as shims that close a gap,"],[261.0,"the drawing defines the final dimension to be achieved, not the shim thickness."],[267.0,"That is exactly the solution from the tolerance stack-up in chapter eight:"],[272.0,"measure at assembly, and select the right shim."],[277.0,"Sometimes a part is not fully machined before assembly."],[281.0,"A hole for a dowel pin, for example, is sometimes drilled through two parts together,"],[287.0,"after they have been assembled, so that they line up exactly."],[292.0,"On the part drawing this appears as a note such as drill at assembly,"],[297.0,"and the dimensions of the hole appear on the assembly drawing."],[302.0,"Someone who drills the hole in advance in the single part, to a dimension,"],[306.0,"loses the match that drilling them together was meant to give."],[311.0,"And there are also dimensions that appear on the assembly as reference dimensions,"],[316.0,"in parentheses, just to give the picture, without requiring inspection."],[322.0,"A large product is usually built like a tree: a top- level assembly, subassemblies, and parts."],[331.0,"Each level has its own drawing and parts list,"],[334.0,"and a subassembly appears in the top-level assembly as a single item."],[339.0,"So when you look for a particular part, you sometimes have to go down several levels."],[345.0,"An exploded view, in which the parts are drawn separated along the assembly line,"],[350.0,"helps you understand the order, but it does not replace the parts list."],[355.0,"And for parts that come in pairs, right-hand and left-hand,"],[359.0,"the drawing sometimes shows only one of them, and states that the other is its mirror image."],[367.0,"A summary of the traps in an assembly drawing."],[371.0,"A balloon with no row in the list, or a quantity that does not match."],[375.0,"A standard screw replaced with a different property class."],[380.0,"A torque without a dry or lubricated thread condition."],[384.0,"A part marked drill at assembly, drilled in advance."],[387.0,"And a shim whose thickness was set in advance, instead of by measurement."],[393.0,"To read an assembly drawing, start from the title block and the parts list."],[398.0,"Then go through every balloon, and make sure it appears in the list and that the quantity is right."],[405.0,"Then read the assembly notes: torques, conditions, sequence, consumables."],[411.0,"And finally the dimensions and fits to be achieved after assembly,"],[415.0,"and whether there is any drilling or machining done together."],[420.0,"Someone who starts assembling before reading the notes may find out"],[424.0,"at the end that the assembly sequence was part of the requirement."],[430.0,"An assembly drawing defines what, how many,"],[433.0,"in which order and with what force, not the geometry of each part."],[438.0,"The parts list and the balloons must match each other, item by item."],[443.0,"A tightening torque always comes with a condition, and sometimes with a sequence."],[450.0,"Adjustment and shims are defined by the final dimension, not by thickness."],[455.0,"And drilling at assembly is done only after the parts have been assembled."],[460.0,"In the next chapter we will get to know drawings for other processes:"],[465.0,"casting, forging, sheet metal and plastics. See you in chapter eighteen."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch18"]={"t":"Casting, Forging, Sheet Metal, Plastics","l":[[1.0,"Until now we have read mostly drawings of machined parts."],[5.0,"But many parts are not born on a machine tool."],[9.0,"Casting, forging, sheet-metal bending and plastic injection molding"],[15.0,"create the shape in a mold or die, not with a cutting tool."],[20.0,"So their drawings include information that does not exist on a machining drawing:"],[26.0,"draft angles, parting lines, radii, shrinkage and machining allowances."],[31.0,"In this chapter we will learn to read this information,"],[35.0,"and what it tells the toolmaker, and whoever machines the part afterwards."],[41.0,"On a cast part, every wall parallel to the direction in which the part is"],[46.0,"withdrawn from the mold needs a small draft, so the part can come out."],[51.0,"The draft usually appears in a general note,"],[54.0,"for example a draft of one to three degrees on all walls, not in every view."],[61.0,"The parting line, where the two halves of the mold meet,"],[64.0,"is marked on the drawing, because a seam can remain along it."],[68.0,"And the radii also usually appear in a note, for example all unspecified radii three millimeters."],[76.0,"A surface to be machined after casting gets a machining allowance,"],[81.0,"and ISO 8062-3 defines both the allowance and the casting tolerance grades."],[88.0,"Sometimes there are two drawings: one of the raw casting and one of the machined part."],[94.0,"material to be removed is drawn with a phantom line."],[101.0,"And raw surfaces are measured from datum targets, as we learned in chapter nine,"],[106.0,"because they are not true enough to serve as a full datum surface."],[112.0,"Forging also has draft and a parting line, but two more things as well."],[117.0,"The first is flash: the excess material squeezed out between the two halves of the die."],[123.0,"The drawing defines how much of it may remain after trimming."],[128.0,"The second is mismatch: the distance by which one half of the part"],[133.0,"is offset relative to the other half, along the parting line."],[138.0,"Both have tolerances from standards such as EN 10243-1, and they usually appear in a note."],[147.0,"And sometimes the grain flow in the material is marked as well,"],[151.0,"because forging orients the grain flow, and that affects strength."],[157.0,"Someone who machines a forged part without knowing where the parting"],[161.0,"line runs may fixture it on the seam, and get an unstable location."],[166.0,"On a sheet-metal part, the drawing usually shows the formed part,"],[171.0,"and sometimes the flat pattern as well."],[174.0,"The bend radius is written explicitly, because it determines"],[178.0,"the length of the flat pattern and the risk of cracking."],[182.0,"It is important to check which side of the bend a dimension is taken to:"],[187.0,"the inside face or the outside face. The difference is the material thickness."],[193.0,"On the flat pattern, the bend lines are marked, with the direction next to them: bend up or bend down."],[201.0,"The rolling direction of the sheet is sometimes marked,"],[204.0,"because bending parallel to the rolling direction can cause cracks."],[209.0,"And a hole too close to a bend line can distort. So the drawing"],[214.0,"sometimes defines a minimum distance, or the hole is made after bending."],[220.0,"machining, and are usually defined in a general note,"],[227.0,"to a standard such as ISO 2768 or a dedicated sheet-metal standard."],[233.0,"On an injection-molded plastic part, much of the definition is aimed at the mold."],[239.0,"Here too there are draft angles and a parting line,"],[243.0,"plus a requirement for uniform wall thickness, so the part cools without warping."],[249.0,"The drawing sometimes defines the gate location, or an area where it may be placed."],[255.0,"allowed, because they leave small circles on the surface."],[262.0,"The dimensions on the drawing are the dimensions of the finished part."],[266.0,"The toolmaker adds the shrinkage of the plastic on cooling himself."],[270.0,"Tolerances for molded parts are defined in ISO 20457,"],[275.0,"which replaced the German standard DIN 16742."],[280.0,"And the surface texture is sometimes defined by a mold finish standard, such as SPI or VDI 3400."],[290.0,"Some plastics absorb moisture, such as nylon."],[294.0,"Their dimensions change with humidity, so the drawing defines when,"],[298.0,"and in what condition, they are measured."],[302.0,"A note such as measure after conditioning for twenty-four"],[306.0,"hours at twenty-three degrees is part of the requirement."],[310.0,"Someone who measures a part right after molding will get different dimensions,"],[315.0,"and may reject a good part or accept a bad one."],[319.0,"There are a few things common to all these drawings."],[322.0,"A surface left as it came out of the mold is sometimes marked with a"],[327.0,"surface texture symbol with a circle: material removal prohibited."],[332.0,"Dimensions between raw surfaces get wide tolerances,"],[336.0,"and dimensions of machined surfaces get machining tolerances."],[341.0,"So it is important to identify for each dimension whether it is between two raw surfaces,"],[347.0,"between a raw and a machined surface, or between two machined surfaces."],[353.0,"A dimension between a raw surface and a machined one is usually the one that"],[358.0,"determines how much material will be removed, and where the part will be fixtured."],[364.0,"A summary of the traps in drawings for these processes."],[368.0,"Draft that was not taken into account: a dimension measured at the wrong end of a drafted wall."],[375.0,"A parting line or flash that was not identified, and fixturing on the seam."],[380.0,"A sheet-metal dimension measured to the wrong side of the bend."],[385.0,"Bending parallel to the rolling direction, even though the drawing marked the direction."],[390.0,"And measuring a plastic part before the required conditioning."],[395.0,"On such a drawing, start by identifying the process and its general notes."],[401.0,"Then identify the parting line, the direction of withdrawal, and the draft angles."],[407.0,"Then identify which surfaces are raw and which are machined,"],[411.0,"and which points the raw ones are measured from."],[415.0,"And only then move on to the dimensions,"],[417.0,"with a clear understanding of which type of surface each one belongs to."],[422.0,"Someone who reads a casting drawing like a machining drawing will demand accuracy"],[428.0,"the mold cannot deliver, or miss a requirement that exists only in the notes."],[434.0,"On drawings for molded parts, the shape is created in the mold,"],[439.0,"and the drawing adds draft, parting lines, radii and machining allowances."],[445.0,"In forging, flash and mismatch are defined too, and sometimes grain flow."],[450.0,"In sheet metal, the bend radius, the side a dimension is taken to,"],[454.0,"and the rolling direction are part of the requirement."],[458.0,"In plastics, the dimensions are those of the finished part, and the standard is ISO 20457."],[465.0,"For materials that absorb moisture, measure after conditioning."],[471.0,"And in every process, identify for each dimension whether it is raw or machined."],[477.0,"In the next chapter we will deal with critical characteristics and inspection:"],[483.0,"what a KC is, and what a first article inspection is."],[487.0,"See you in chapter nineteen."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch19"]={"t":"Critical Characteristics and Inspection","l":[[1.0,"A drawing has dozens of dimensions, and they are not all equally important."],[6.0,"Some dimensions, if exceeded, still leave a working part. And some,"],[11.0,"if exceeded, make the part fail, and sometimes put people at risk."],[16.0,"The drawing marks these dimensions, and that marking determines how"],[20.0,"they are manufactured, how they are inspected, and what is documented."],[25.0,"In this chapter we will learn to read critical characteristics, and understand what"],[30.0,"first article inspection and measurement on a coordinate measuring machine require."],[36.0,"A key characteristic, a KC, is a dimension or requirement whose"],[41.0,"variation has a strong effect on function, safety or assembly."],[46.0,"diamond, a hexagon, a letter in a box, or another symbol."],[55.0,"So the meaning of the symbol is always defined in the"],[58.0,"notes on the drawing, or in the customer's specification."],[61.0,"Many organizations also have a classification of defects: critical, major and minor."],[68.0,"A critical defect can cause a hazard, a major defect impairs"],[73.0,"the function, and a minor defect does not affect use."],[77.0,"Someone who does not know the symbol reads a critical characteristic like any other dimension."],[84.0,"with a tolerance. It is a requirement for process control."],[91.0,"It usually requires statistical control in production, not just inspection at the end."],[97.0,"Sometimes it requires one hundred percent inspection of the parts, not sampling."],[102.0,"And it requires documentation: who measured, with what,"],[106.0,"and what the result was, for every part or every batch."],[110.0,"And sometimes the process that produces it must not be"],[113.0,"changed without approval, even if the change looks small."],[117.0,"A critical characteristic that passes inspection but was not measured according"],[123.0,"to the procedure, as far as the customer is concerned, was not inspected."],[128.0,"producing a critical characteristic, you measure its capability."],[135.0,"Cp compares the tolerance width to the spread of the process:"],[140.0,"the tolerance width, divided by six standard deviations."],[145.0,"Cpk adds the location of the process: the distance from the"],[149.0,"mean to the nearer limit, divided by three standard deviations."],[155.0,"A process can be narrow enough, but too close to a limit, and then Cp is good and Cpk is low."],[162.0,"A common requirement is a Cpk of at least 1.33, and sometimes more for critical characteristics."],[170.0,"So someone who aims for the edge of the tolerance to leave material"],[175.0,"for rework lowers the Cpk, even when every part is within tolerance."],[180.0,"First article inspection, FAI, proves that the process is capable"],[185.0,"of producing a part that meets every requirement of the drawing."],[190.0,"In aerospace it is defined in AS9102."],[194.0,"The standard defines three forms: the first identifies"],[198.0,"the part and the drawing, the second documents materials,"],[202.0,"special processes and functional tests, and the third documents every single characteristic."],[210.0,"In the third form, every dimension, every tolerance and every"],[214.0,"note on the drawing gets a row, with the measured result."],[218.0,"And this inspection is repeated after a design change, a process change,"],[223.0,"a move to another site, or a long break in production."],[227.0,"Someone who changes the manufacturing process and does not repeat the inspection"],[232.0,"is supplying a part that has not been proven to meet the requirements."],[237.0,"For first article inspection to be complete, the drawing is ballooned."],[241.0,"Every requirement gets a balloon with a number: a dimension,"],[246.0,"a geometric tolerance, a surface texture, and even a general note."],[251.0,"Every number is a row in the inspection report,"],[254.0,"so no requirement can be missed without its row staying empty."],[258.0,"Notes such as edge breaks, part marking or coating also"],[261.0,"get a number, and are inspected like any dimension."],[265.0,"And on a multi-sheet drawing, the numbering runs across all the sheets, not just the first one."],[273.0,"A coordinate measuring machine, a CMM,"],[276.0,"measures points on the part and calculates the geometry from them."],[281.0,"Its program must be built according to the datum reference frame"],[285.0,"on the drawing: the same datum features, in the same order."],[289.0,"A program that builds its axes from other surfaces gives"],[292.0,"numbers that look accurate, but measure something else."],[296.0,"And the calculation method matters too: least squares,"],[300.0,"minimum circumscribed or maximum inscribed cylinder, as we saw in chapter six."],[307.0,"So a CMM report must state the datum reference frame"],[310.0,"and the calculation method, not just the number."],[314.0,"The instrument that measures is also part of the result."],[318.0,"instrument should be at most one tenth of the tolerance width."],[324.0,"And a repeatability and reproducibility study, Gage R&R, checks how much of the spread"],[330.0,"in the measurements comes from the instrument and the operator, not from the parts."],[337.0,"parts and accept bad ones, and nobody will notice."],[344.0,"traceability to a recognized measurement standard."],[350.0,"Every measurement includes uncertainty. When a result is close to the tolerance limit,"],[356.0,"it is hard to know for sure which side of it the part is on."],[360.0,"ISO 14253-1 defines a decision rule: to prove a part conforms,"],[367.0,"the result must be within the tolerance, reduced by the uncertainty."],[375.0,"In other words, the acceptance zone is narrower than the tolerance on the drawing."],[380.0,"In some organizations the decision rule is defined in the contract or on the drawing,"],[386.0,"and in others it is not. When it is not defined, it is worth agreeing on it in advance."],[393.0,"And temperature is part of the measurement too: the dimensions on"],[397.0,"the drawing apply at twenty degrees, as we saw in chapter zero."],[402.0,"A summary of the traps in inspection."],[405.0,"A critical characteristic measured like any other dimension, with no control and no documentation."],[412.0,"First article inspection that is not repeated after a process change."],[416.0,"A measurement program built from datum features other than those on the drawing."],[421.0,"An instrument whose resolution is close to the tolerance width."],[425.0,"And a result right at the limit, accepted without taking uncertainty into account."],[431.0,"Critical characteristics are marked with a symbol defined in the notes,"],[435.0,"and they require control, documentation and sometimes full inspection."],[440.0,"Cpk measures both spread and location, and a common requirement is 1.33."],[447.0,"First article inspection to AS9102 checks every requirement on the"],[451.0,"ballooned drawing, and is repeated after every significant change."],[456.0,"And in the next chapter we will get to know the drawing that has no sheet:"],[461.0,"model-based definition, MBD. See you in chapter twenty."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch20"]={"t":"Model-Based Definition \u00b7 MBD","l":[[1.0,"In every chapter so far, the definition of the part has been a sheet: views, dimensions and notes."],[10.0,"In many organizations this is changing. The definition moves into the"],[15.0,"3D model itself, and the 2D drawing disappears or becomes secondary."],[20.0,"This is called model-based definition, MBD."],[24.0,"In this chapter we will learn to read such a model,"],[27.0,"and what changes in the rules we have learned, when the model is the contract."],[34.0,"be on the drawing is attached to the geometry itself."],[42.0,"Dimensions, geometric tolerances, datums, surface texture and notes"],[47.0,"are together called PMI: product and manufacturing information."],[52.0,"There are two kinds of PMI. Graphical PMI looks like text on the screen,"],[57.0,"but the computer does not know which surface it belongs to."],[62.0,"Semantic PMI is linked to the surfaces themselves, and the computer knows"],[67.0,"which tolerance applies to which surface, relative to which datums."],[73.0,"Only semantic PMI lets CAM software or a measuring"],[77.0,"machine read the requirements automatically."],[81.0,"Y14.41, and on the international side in ISO 16792."],[91.0,"The rules for such a model are defined in ASME Y14.41, and on the international side in ISO 16792."],[92.0,"The symbols themselves have not changed: the same GD&T symbols,"],[97.0,"the same frames and the same datums, only in 3D space."],[102.0,"To transfer a model with PMI between different systems, the STEP AP242 format is used."],[111.0,"And for inspection results and measurement plans there is the QIF format,"],[116.0,"which links the requirement in the model to the measured result."],[121.0,"An MBD model usually includes saved views, which correspond to the views on a drawing."],[127.0,"Each saved view shows a particular viewing direction, and only the PMI"],[132.0,"that belongs to it, so that not everything appears on the screen at once."],[138.0,"When you select a tolerance, the system highlights the surface it applies to and its datum features."],[145.0,"So in a good model there is no question which surface a note belongs to,"],[150.0,"as there sometimes is on a crowded drawing."],[153.0,"Someone who reads a model only through a picture of it loses all this information."],[160.0,"Here there is a fundamental change compared to a drawing."],[164.0,"In chapter zero we learned not to measure from the picture."],[168.0,"In an MBD model, the geometry is the definition, and it is exact."],[173.0,"A dimension that is not written explicitly is queried from the model, as a basic dimension."],[180.0,"And its tolerance comes from a general note, usually a profile of a"],[185.0,"surface tolerance on all surfaces, relative to a datum reference frame."],[190.0,"For example: all unmarked surfaces, a profile of five tenths relative to A, B and C."],[199.0,"In other words, in an MBD model every surface is defined, even if there is no number next to it."],[207.0,"When the model is the contract, its version matters exactly as much as a drawing revision."],[214.0,"The file must be identified with a number and a version,"],[217.0,"and often with an integrity check of the file as well."],[221.0,"Many organizations add a reduced drawing: a 2D sheet with the important dimensions,"],[228.0,"the material and the notes, for convenience on the shop floor."],[234.0,"But the reduced drawing is usually for reference only, and the model governs."],[240.0,"So it is important to check what is stated about the relationship between them,"],[245.0,"and which one governs when they contradict each other."],[249.0,"Not every 3D file carries the same information."],[253.0,"A native CAD file carries everything, but requires the same system."],[258.0,"STEP AP242 carries exact geometry and semantic PMI as well."],[265.0,"Older versions of STEP, such as AP203 or AP214,"],[269.0,"usually carry only geometry, without the requirements."],[275.0,"And triangle mesh files, such as STL,"],[278.0,"are an approximation of the geometry, not the geometry itself."],[283.0,"Someone who receives a model in a format that does not carry PMI receives"],[289.0,"a shape without tolerances, and that is not enough for manufacturing."],[295.0,"The great advantage of MBD is that the same model can be used all the way through."],[300.0,"The CAM software builds toolpaths directly from the geometry."],[305.0,"The measuring machine can read the semantic PMI and build a measurement program from it."],[311.0,"And first article inspection can be built directly from the list of"],[315.0,"requirements in the model, instead of from manual ballooning of a drawing."],[320.0,"But this requires a tool at every station that can display the model and its PMI."],[326.0,"A worker who has only a picture or a reduced drawing does not see some of the requirements."],[333.0,"A summary of the traps in an MBD model."],[336.0,"Measuring a surface from the model when its tolerance is"],[340.0,"defined elsewhere, without checking the general note."],[344.0,"Working to an old version of the model, because the file was saved on a local computer."],[350.0,"Saving to a format that does not carry PMI, and losing all the tolerances."],[356.0,"A reduced drawing that was not updated after a change to the model."],[360.0,"And units: a model built in inches and opened as if it were in millimeters."],[366.0,"Start by identifying the model: number, version, and the status of the reduced drawing."],[374.0,"Then the general notes: units, standard, and the general profile tolerance."],[379.0,"Then the saved views, one by one, with the PMI of each."],[384.0,"And only then queries on the geometry, for dimensions that are not written explicitly."],[390.0,"Someone who starts by measuring the model, without reading the notes,"],[394.0,"does not know which tolerance applies to what he measured."],[399.0,"In MBD, the 3D model is the definition, and the PMI is linked to the geometry."],[405.0,"requirements, and STEP AP242 transfers it between systems."],[413.0,"Dimensions that are not written are taken from the model,"],[417.0,"and their tolerance comes from a general note."],[420.0,"And the model version matters like a revision, and the model governs over the reduced drawing."],[426.0,"In the next chapter we will deal with change management:"],[431.0,"revisions, change orders, and deviations."],[435.0,"See you in chapter twenty-one."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch21"]={"t":"Change Management","l":[[1.0,"mistakes are discovered, and requirements change."],[8.0,"And with every change, the important question is not only what changed,"],[13.0,"but whether everyone is working to the same definition."],[17.0,"Change management is the system that ensures this: who requests, who approves,"],[22.0,"from when the change applies, and what is done with what has already been made."],[28.0,"In this chapter we will learn to read a drawing that has changed,"],[32.0,"and to tell a revision, a deviation and a waiver apart."],[38.0,"A drawing that has changed has a revision block, usually in the upper corner."],[43.0,"Each row has a revision letter or number,"],[46.0,"a short description of the change, a date, and who approved it."],[51.0,"The rules for this are defined in ASME Y14.35."],[56.0,"Where something changed in the picture, a small triangle with"],[60.0,"the revision letter sometimes appears, so it is easy to find."],[65.0,"And around the border of the sheet there is a grid of letters and numbers,"],[69.0,"like on a map. The revision block states which zone each change is in."],[73.0,"Someone who reads only the latest revision in the block,"],[77.0,"and does not look for the markers in the picture, may miss a change that looks small."],[84.0,"Not every change gets a new revision. Sometimes it gets a new part number."],[89.0,"The rule is interchangeability: can an old part and a new part replace each other everywhere?"],[95.0,"the parts are interchangeable, and a revision is enough."],[103.0,"If the change affects one of them, for example a hole that moved and"],[107.0,"will not fit an old assembly, the new part gets a new part number."],[112.0,"Otherwise, two different parts with the same number will"],[115.0,"sit in the stores, and someone will assemble the wrong one."],[120.0,"A change starts with an engineering change request, an ECR,"],[124.0,"which explains what the problem is and what is proposed."],[129.0,"After approval, an engineering change order, an ECO, is issued,"],[133.0,"which defines exactly what changes and in which documents."],[138.0,"The order also defines effectivity: from when the change applies."],[144.0,"For example from a certain serial number, from a date, or from a production lot."],[151.0,"And it defines the disposition of what already exists:"],[155.0,"parts in stock, parts in work, and parts at the customer."],[160.0,"The options are usually: use as is, rework, or scrap."],[164.0,"Someone who receives a drawing at a new revision without checking the effectivity may"],[169.0,"stop production that is still allowed, or continue production that is no longer allowed."],[175.0,"There are situations in which the drawing is not changed, and yet it is not met."],[181.0,"A deviation is approval in advance to manufacture differently"],[184.0,"from the drawing, for a limited quantity or a limited time."],[189.0,"For example, a different material because the material on"],[193.0,"the drawing is unavailable, for one hundred parts only."],[197.0,"A waiver is approval after the fact to accept a part that"],[201.0,"has already been made and does not meet the requirement."],[205.0,"In some standards it is also called a concession."],[209.0,"In both cases, the drawing does not change."],[212.0,"The approval applies only to the parts or the period defined in it."],[217.0,"Someone who keeps manufacturing under a deviation after"],[221.0,"the quantity has run out is producing nonconforming parts."],[226.0,"When a part does not meet a requirement, a material review board decides what to do with it."],[233.0,"Rework brings the part into full conformance with the drawing. After rework, the part conforms."],[240.0,"Repair leaves the part different from the drawing, but usable."],[244.0,"So repair requires engineering approval, and sometimes customer approval as well."],[250.0,"Someone who calls a repair rework is hiding the fact that the part does not meet the drawing."],[257.0,"When working with a customer, there are changes the customer must approve."],[262.0,"A change that affects form, fit, function, safety or cost is"],[267.0,"considered a Class | change, and requires customer approval."],[272.0,"A minor change, such as correcting a spelling mistake,"],[277.0,"is considered a Class II change, and sometimes a notification is enough."],[283.0,"And a process change too, without a change to the drawing, can require approval,"],[288.0,"as we Saw with first article inspection in chapter nineteen."],[294.0,"On the shop floor, the important question is which copy is in your hands."],[299.0,"An old printed copy is one of the most common sources of mistakes. So printed copies are"],[305.0,"sometimes marked as uncontrolled copies, or collected when a new revision is issued."],[311.0,"A handwritten correction on a drawing, without a change order, is not a change. It is only a note."],[318.0,"against the system, not just against the sheet in your hands."],[326.0,"A summary of the traps in change management."],[329.0,"Working from an old copy, because it was the one available next to the machine."],[334.0,"A handwritten correction treated as an approved change."],[337.0,"A deviation used after its quantity or its period has run out."],[342.0,"A new revision, where a new part number was needed."],[345.0,"Old stock that was not dealt with, because nobody read the disposition."],[350.0,"And a process change, without repeating first article inspection."],[355.0,"When you receive a drawing that has changed,"],[358.0,"start from the revision block: what changed, and in which zone."],[364.0,"Then look for the revision markers in the picture, and compare with the previous revision."],[369.0,"Then read the change order: the effectivity, and the disposition of what already exists."],[375.0,"And finally check that the related documents were updated too:"],[380.0,"work instructions, inspection plans and machining programs."],[384.0,"Someone who updates only the drawing, and keeps the old measurement program,"],[389.0,"will measure a new part to old requirements."],[394.0,"The revision block, the markers in the picture and the"],[397.0,"zones around the border show what changed and where."],[402.0,"A change that breaks interchangeability gets a new part number, not just a revision."],[408.0,"A deviation is approval in advance, a waiver is approval after the fact,"],[413.0,"and both apply only to what is defined in them."],[417.0,"Rework brings the part to the drawing, and repair leaves it different and requires approval."],[423.0,"In the next chapter we move to the last part of the course,"],[427.0,"and read a drawing through the manufacturer's eyes. See you in chapter twenty-two."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch22"]={"t":"Reading Through the Manufacturer's Eyes","l":[[1.0,"Until now we have read drawings to understand what the designer requires."],[6.0,"The manufacturer reads the same drawing with a different question:"],[10.0,"how do we make this, how much will it cost, and what can go wrong."],[15.0,"This kind of reading is done before the quote and before work starts,"],[19.0,"not after the part is already on the machine."],[22.0,"In this chapter we will go through the drawing in the order an experienced"],[27.0,"manufacturer reads it, and see what to look for at each stage."],[31.0,"The first pass is the title block: material, condition, quantity, revision and standard."],[39.0,"The material and the overall dimensions determine the stock:"],[43.0,"which bar diameter, which plate thickness, and how much machining allowance."],[48.0,"A material that is not available in the right size changes"],[53.0,"the price and the lead time before work even starts."],[57.0,"coating, marking, and documents the drawing refers to."],[62.0,"Every such reference is additional work, and sometimes an outside supplier and waiting time."],[69.0,"The next question is how many setups will be needed."],[73.0,"Every side of the part that has machined features is"],[76.0,"usually another setup, and every setup has setup time."],[79.0,"The datum reference frame says what to locate on: the primary datum feature"],[84.0,"is usually machined first, and serves as the basis for everything else."],[89.0,"Features with a tight tolerance between them are best machined"],[93.0,"in the same setup, so as not to introduce a transfer error."],[97.0,"When the drawing requires a tight relationship between two sides of the part,"],[101.0,"you need to plan how the datum is transferred between setups."],[106.0,"After that, look for what determines the process and the cost."],[110.0,"The tightest tolerance on the drawing sometimes determines the entire process:"],[115.0,"grinding, reaming, or a separate finishing operation."],[120.0,"A low roughness, or a very small geometric tolerance, also adds an operation."],[126.0,"A sharp internal corner in a pocket is a well-known trap."],[130.0,"An end mill always leaves a radius, and a truly sharp corner requires EDM."],[136.0,"So when there is no internal radius on the drawing, ask which radius is allowed, before quoting."],[143.0,"A deep, narrow hole, with a depth of more than ten times the diameter,"],[147.0,"requires a special deep-hole drill, with through-tool coolant, and a long time."],[153.0,"And thin walls distort during machining and clamping, and need to be planned for in advance."],[160.0,"A long, slender shaft, with a length of more than four times"],[164.0,"the diameter, will deflect in turning without support."],[168.0,"It requires a tailstock center or a steady rest, and that affects the setup and the time."],[174.0,"chatters and limits the speed and the surface finish."],[182.0,"A deep internal thread in a blind hole, a narrow groove,"],[186.0,"or a very small radius: each of them requires a special tool."],[192.0,"A manufacturer who identifies all of these in advance quotes correctly and plans correctly."],[198.0,"A manufacturer who discovers them on the machine loses money."],[203.0,"As we saw in chapter thirteen, the notes define a sequence of operations."],[208.0,"Heat treatment in the middle of the process requires leaving a"],[212.0,"machining allowance, and planning finish machining after it."],[216.0,"Coating at the end of the process requires knowing whether the dimensions"],[220.0,"are before or after coating, and masking the areas that must not be coated."],[225.0,"Every such outside step adds lead time, so it is part of the planning and of the price."],[232.0,"An experienced manufacturer also checks how he will"],[235.0,"measure every dimension, before he makes anything."],[239.0,"Does he have an instrument with enough resolution for the tightest tolerance?"],[243.0,"Are there critical characteristics that require statistical control or inspection of every part?"],[249.0,"Is first article inspection required, and what does it include?"],[254.0,"And when inspection requires equipment he does not have,"],[257.0,"he needs to decide in advance whether to send it out for inspection, or to ask."],[264.0,"From this whole pass, a list of questions for the designer is built."],[268.0,"Dimensions that contradict each other, missing tolerances,"],[272.0,"an undefined datum feature, or a coating with no thickness."],[277.0,"Features that cannot be made as drawn, such as a sharp internal corner."],[282.0,"And sometimes suggestions too: a larger radius, a wider tolerance"],[286.0,"that does not harm the function, or a more available material."],[291.0,"But a suggestion is a question, not a decision. As we Saw in chapter zero,"],[297.0,"a manufacturer does not change the drawing on his own."],[302.0,"And a question asked before manufacturing costs much less"],[305.0,"than one discovered after the part has been rejected."],[309.0,"In a quote, the drawing is translated into time and material."],[314.0,"The setup time for each setup is spread over the whole quantity,"],[318.0,"so in a small quantity it is a large part of the price."],[322.0,"The cycle time per part is determined mainly by the tolerances,"],[326.0,"the surface finish and the amount of material removed."],[330.0,"And special processes, such as heat treatment,"],[333.0,"coating and testing, come with their own price and lead time."],[338.0,"Someone who quotes by geometry alone, without the notes, loses money on every part."],[345.0,"A summary of the traps in reading through the manufacturer's eyes."],[349.0,"A quote without reading the notes and the referenced documents."],[353.0,"Assuming the general tolerance is loose, without checking the title block."],[358.0,"A sharp internal corner, or a deep hole, discovered only on the machine."],[364.0,"and get asked only after the part has been rejected."],[371.0,"The manufacturer reads the title block and the notes first,"],[375.0,"and determines the stock, the material and the outside processes."],[380.0,"The datum reference frame determines the setups,"],[382.0,"and tight relationships are machined in the same setup."],[386.0,"difficult features determine the process and the cost."],[395.0,"And every ambiguity becomes a question for the designer, before manufacturing, not after."],[401.0,"In the next chapter we will practice everything we have learned ina"],[405.0,"diagnostic workshop: drawings with errors, and we will find them together."],[411.0,"See you in chapter twenty-three."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch23"]={"t":"Diagnostic Workshop","l":[[1.0,"In this chapter we will not learn anything new. We will use what we have learned."],[6.0,"We will look at six drawings, and each of them has one error."],[11.0,"After each drawing we will pause for a moment. Try to find the error yourself, before we reveal it."],[18.0,"And for every error we will ask three questions: what is wrong,"],[22.0,"what will come out of it in production, and what do we ask the designer."],[28.0,"Here is a plate with three dimensions: forty, eighty, and one hundred twenty for the overall length."],[35.0,"Each of them has a tolerance of plus or minus one tenth. What is wrong?"],[41.0,"The dimensions close a chain. The overall length is defined twice:"],[46.0,"once directly, and once as the sum of the other two dimensions."],[51.0,"If the two smaller dimensions are at their upper limit, the sum is one hundred twenty point two,"],[56.0,"and that is already outside the tolerance of the overall dimension."],[61.0,"The manufacturer does not know which dimension to honor."],[64.0,"So we ask the designer which of them is a reference dimension, in parentheses."],[71.0,"Here is a position tolerance frame, referring to datums A and B."],[75.0,"Only datum A is identified in the picture. What is wrong?"],[80.0,"Datum B is not defined anywhere on the drawing, so the tolerance is incomplete."],[87.0,"Every manufacturer will choose a different surface, and every measuring machine"],[92.0,"will build a different reference frame. The results will not be comparable."],[98.0,"The question for the designer is simple: which feature is datum B?"],[104.0,"Here is a hole with a position tolerance of one tenth, relative to A, B and C."],[111.0,"Its location is defined by dimensions of thirty and twenty,"],[115.0,"with a tolerance of plus or minus five hundredths. What is wrong?"],[121.0,"With a position tolerance, the dimensions that locate the"],[124.0,"feature must be basic dimensions, in a frame, with no tolerance."],[129.0,"Here there are two contradicting definitions: a square zone from"],[133.0,"the dimensions, and a round zone from the geometric tolerance."],[138.0,"The manufacturer does not know which to inspect. The question for the designer:"],[143.0,"are the dimensions supposed to be basic dimensions?"],[148.0,"Here is a hole with an H7 fit, and a note: zinc plating, eight microns."],[154.0,"There is no note about a masked area, and no note on whether"],[158.0,"the dimensions are before or after plating. What is wrong?"],[163.0,"Eight microns of plating on each side reduces the hole by sixteen microns,"],[168.0,"and that is almost the entire H7 tolerance width at this diameter."],[173.0,"If the part is machined to the drawing and then plated, the hole will come out of tolerance."],[180.0,"The question for the designer: should the hole be masked during plating,"],[184.0,"or does the dimension apply after plating?"],[188.0,"Here is an M8 thread, sixteen deep, in a blind hole."],[193.0,"The tap drill depth is also defined as sixteen. What is wrong?"],[198.0,"The tap drill hole must be deeper than the thread, to leave room for the tip of the tap."],[204.0,"At equal depths, the thread will not be full to the bottom, or the tap will hit the bottom and break."],[210.0,"The question for the designer: what is the tap drill depth, or what full-thread depth is really required?"],[218.0,"Here is an ISO fillet weld symbol, with a double reference line,"],[222.0,"and the number five to the left of the triangle."],[226.0,"What is wrong?"],[227.0,"In ISO, a letter must appear before the number: a for throat thickness, or z for leg length."],[234.0,"Without a letter, five can be a throat thickness of five, meaning a leg of about seven, or a leg of five."],[242.0,"The difference is about thirty percent in strength. The question for the designer: a or z?"],[250.0,"In each of these cases, the drawing looks complete at first glance."],[255.0,"The error was not in the picture, but in the relationship between two details:"],[262.0,"a dimension and a dimension, a tolerance and a datum, a fit and a coating."],[269.0,"but checking that the details agree with each other."],[275.0,"In every case, the solution was not to choose an interpretation, but to ask."],[281.0,"A manufacturer who chooses an interpretation on his own takes on the responsibility for the result."],[288.0,"A good question for the designer is short and unambiguous."],[292.0,"It states the drawing number, the revision, and the location on the sheet, by the zone on the border."],[299.0,"It describes the problem in one sentence, and offers one or two options."],[305.0,"For example: on drawing number such-and-such, revision C,"],[310.0,"zone B3, datum B is not defined. Is the bottom surface intended?"],[316.0,"A question like that is answered quickly, and leaves no room for interpretation."],[322.0,"And a question that was asked and answered in writing is"],[325.0,"kept with the drawing, so the next production run knows too."],[329.0,"A summary of the traps in the workshop."],[332.0,"Reading each detail on its own, and not checking the relationship between them."],[337.0,"Choosing a convenient interpretation, instead of asking."],[341.0,"Asking verbally, without documenting."],[344.0,"And correcting the drawing ourselves, by hand, as if it were an approved change."],[350.0,"We saw Six errors: a closed chain, a missing datum, position with ordinary dimensions,"],[357.0,"plating on a fit, a short tap drill hole, and a weld with no letter."],[363.0,"In all of them the error was in the relationship between details,"],[367.0,"and in all of them the solution was a question."],[371.0,"In the next chapter, the last in the course, we will bring everything we"],[375.0,"have learned together into a complete reading protocol, step by step."],[381.0,"See you in chapter twenty-four."]]};
window.MH_TR["ch24"]={"t":"A Complete Reading Protocol","l":[[1.0,"This is the last chapter of the course."],[4.0,"Throughout the course we learned each part of the drawing separately:"],[8.0,"views, dimensions, tolerances, datums, materials and documents."],[13.0,"In this chapter we will bring it all together into one protocol, ten steps, that suits any drawing."],[20.0,"The goal is not to skip any step, and not to discover a"],[24.0,"problem only when the part is already on the machine."],[28.0,"The first step is to check what is in your hands."],[31.0,"The drawing number, the revision, and the number of sheets."],[35.0,"The revision must match the one on the order."],[38.0,"And also check the document status and the approvals:"],[42.0,"a drawing that has not been released is still a draft."],[46.0,"The second step is to read the rules the drawing was written by."],[50.0,"The units, the projection symbol, and the tolerancing standard,"],[54.0,"which determines whether the envelope principle or the independency principle applies."],[60.0,"And then the general tolerances and the general notes."],[64.0,"They apply to every dimension that has no tolerance of its own."],[69.0,"The third step is the material and its condition, the heat treatment, and the coating."],[74.0,"And with them, all the documents the drawing refers to:"],[78.0,"material specifications, process specifications and inspection standards."],[84.0,"From here you can already know what the stock is,"],[87.0,"which outside processes will be needed, and in which order."],[92.0,"The fourth step is to understand the shape."],[94.0,"Go through the views and the sections, and build a 3D picture of the part in your head."],[100.0,"And pay attention to conventions and simplifications, such as threads,"],[105.0,"symmetrical parts and hidden lines, so you do not read them as ordinary geometry."],[113.0,"The fifth step is the datum reference frame."],[116.0,"Identify the primary, secondary and tertiary datums, and the order between them."],[123.0,"The datum reference frame determines how the part is set"],[126.0,"up in manufacturing, and how it is fixtured in inspection."],[130.0,"The sixth step is to go through every dimension."],[134.0,"For every dimension, check what type it is: a toleranced dimension,"],[139.0,"a basic dimension, or a reference dimension."],[142.0,"Check the dimension chains, and that no dimension is defined twice."],[147.0,"whether it is a clearance, transition or interference fit."],[156.0,"The seventh step is to go through every feature control frame."],[160.0,"In every frame ask: what does it apply to, what is the shape of the zone,"],[166.0,"relative to which datums, and is there an MMC or LMC modifier."],[171.0,"And check that every datum that appears in the frame is actually identified on the drawing."],[177.0,"The eighth step is everything defined by symbols and references."],[182.0,"tap drill holes, welds, edges, undercuts and splines."],[191.0,"This is where many of the errors we saw in the"],[193.0,"diagnostic workshop are found, because they look small."],[198.0,"The ninth step is to identify critical characteristics, and plan the inspection."],[203.0,"Which dimensions are marked as critical, and what they require:"],[208.0,"control, documentation, or inspection of every part."],[212.0,"Is first article inspection required, and is there a suitable measuring instrument for every tolerance."],[220.0,"The tenth step is to collect everything that is unclear."],[224.0,"Every contradiction, every missing tolerance,"],[227.0,"and every feature that cannot be made as drawn, becomes a question."],[231.0,"The question includes the drawing number, the revision and the zone,"],[235.0,"and it is sent to the designer before work starts."],[239.0,"And the answers are kept in writing, with the drawing."],[243.0,"On an assembly drawing, add the parts list, the balloons,"],[247.0,"the assembly notes and the torques to the protocol."],[251.0,"In an MBD model, the steps are the same,"],[254.0,"but they are read from the saved views and the PMI, not from a sheet."],[259.0,"And on a drawing that has changed, start from the revision block,"],[263.0,"and check what changed and what the effectivity is."],[267.0,"You might ask why a protocol is needed, if you know how to read a drawing."],[272.0,"The answer is that most of the mistakes we saw in this course did"],[276.0,"not come from a lack of knowledge, but from a step that was skipped."],[281.0,"A revision that was not checked, a general note that was not read,"],[285.0,"or a datum that nobody noticed was missing."],[288.0,"A fixed protocol turns reading into a routine, and routine is what prevents mistakes."],[295.0,"In this course we started from the drawing as a contract, and went through its whole language."],[301.0,"Views and sections, dimensions and tolerances, fits and stack-ups,"],[305.0,"datum reference frames and geometric tolerances."],[309.0,"Surface texture, welds, materials and threads, and then the drawing as a document:"],[317.0,"title block, assembly, processes, inspection, the model and changes."],[325.0,"And at the end, reading through the manufacturer's eyes,"],[328.0,"a diagnostic workshop, and one protocol that ties it all together."],[333.0,"A drawing is a contract. Whoever reads it completely"],[336.0,"makes what is required, and asks before making a mistake."],[341.0,"Thank you for learning with us."],[343.0,"mechanical engineering material, can be found at METAL HUB."],[350.0,"Good luck reading your next drawing."],[352.0,"Goodbye."]]};
